window.NOTES_DATA = {"generated": true, "subjects": [{"id": "ds", "name": "Data Structures", "short": "DS", "icon": "&#127794;", "color": "#5b6cff", "blurb": "Arrays, searching, sorting, stacks, queues and complexity analysis.", "sort": 1}, {"id": "oop", "name": "Object Oriented Programming", "short": "OOP", "icon": "&#129513;", "color": "#ff7a59", "blurb": "C++ fundamentals, classes, inheritance, polymorphism and memory.", "sort": 2}, {"id": "coa", "name": "Computer Organization & Architecture", "short": "COA", "icon": "&#129504;", "color": "#17b3a3", "blurb": "Number systems, computer arithmetic, CPU design, CISC vs RISC.", "sort": 3}, {"id": "it", "name": "IT & Python Programming", "short": "IT", "icon": "&#128013;", "color": "#f0a92c", "blurb": "Python basics, data types, operators, control flow and strings.", "sort": 4}, {"id": "ob", "name": "Organizational Behaviour", "short": "OB", "icon": "&#128101;", "color": "#a963d6", "blurb": "Individual behaviour, personality, emotions, perception and leadership.", "sort": 5}, {"id": "math", "name": "Mathematics - Probability", "short": "MATH", "icon": "&#128208;", "color": "#2bb6dc", "blurb": "Probability, Bayes' theorem, random variables, expectation and variance.", "sort": 6}], "notes": [{"id": "coa-u1", "title": "COA Unit 1 — Data Representation, Computer Arithmetic & Memory", "subject": "coa", "unit": 1, "description": "Computer systems, organization vs architecture, Von Neumann design, computer generations; fixed point, floating point and decimal arithmetic; Booth's multiplication algorithm; memory hierarchy, memory types and the TLB — with flowcharts and a hardware implementation diagram.", "tags": "von neumann, binary, booth algorithm, floating point, memory hierarchy, tlb, mips", "date": "2026-02-11", "featured": 1, "words": 2521, "reading_min": 13, "views": 298, "body_html": "<h2 id=\"introduction-to-computer-systems\">Introduction to Computer Systems<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#introduction-to-computer-systems\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">computer system</mark> is a combination of hardware and software that accepts <strong>data</strong>, processes it, stores it and produces <strong>information</strong>. It has four basic functional units:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Input unit</strong> — accepts instructions and data (keyboard, mouse, scanner).</li>\n<li><strong>Memory unit</strong> — stores data and programs (registers, cache, RAM, secondary storage).</li>\n<li><strong>ALU (Arithmetic and Logic Unit)</strong> — performs arithmetic and logical operations.</li>\n<li><strong>Control unit (CU)</strong> — directs the sequence of operations; CU + ALU + registers = the <strong>CPU</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Output unit</strong> — presents results (monitor, printer).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"flow\"> <div class=\"fnode\">Input unit</div> <div class=\"farrow\">→</div> <div class=\"fnode fmain\">Memory unit (stores program + data)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↕</div> <div class=\"fnode\">ALU &nbsp;·&nbsp; Control unit (registers) — CPU</div> <div class=\"farrow\">→</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Output unit</div> </div>\n<p>Two other definitions you must know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><mark class=\"kw\">Bit</mark> — one binary digit (0 or 1); the smallest unit of data.</li>\n<li><mark class=\"kw\">Byte</mark> — a group of 8 bits; the standard unit of memory.</li>\n<li><strong>Word</strong> — a fixed-size group of bits the CPU handles as a unit (usually 16, 32 or 64 bits).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Approximate size</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 KB</td><td>2<sup>10</sup> = 1,024 bytes</td></tr><tr><td>1 MB</td><td>2<sup>20</sup> ≈ 1.05 × 10<sup>6</sup> bytes</td></tr><tr><td>1 GB</td><td>2<sup>30</sup> ≈ 1.07 × 10<sup>9</sup> bytes</td></tr><tr><td>1 TB</td><td>2<sup>40</sup> ≈ 1.10 × 10<sup>12</sup> bytes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"organization-vs-architecture\">Organization vs Architecture<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#organization-vs-architecture\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Computer <strong>Organization</strong></th><th>Computer <strong>Architecture</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Concern</td><td><strong>How</strong> the hardware is implemented</td><td><strong>What</strong> the machine looks like to a programmer</td></tr><tr><td>Deals with</td><td>data path, control signals, memory technology, I/O circuits</td><td>instruction set, addressing modes, word size, registers</td></tr><tr><td>Question</td><td><em>How do we build it?</em></td><td><em>What features does it have?</em></td></tr><tr><td>Changeable</td><td>often (technologies change)</td><td>rarely (software depends on it)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Two machines with the <em>same architecture</em> (same instruction set) can have <em>different organizations</em> — one fast, one cheap. Software written for one runs on both.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"von-neumann-architecture\">Von Neumann Architecture<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#von-neumann-architecture\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Proposed by <strong>John von Neumann (1945)</strong>, it is built on the <mark class=\"kw\">stored-program concept</mark>: <strong>both the program (instructions) and the data are stored together in the same memory</strong>, and the CPU fetches instructions one at a time.</p>\n<div class=\"flow\"> <div class=\"fnode fmain\">Main memory — stores instructions + data (single address space)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↕</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Control unit</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↕</div> <div class=\"fnode\">ALU &nbsp;·&nbsp; Registers (AC, MBR, MAR, PC, IR)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↑ ↓</div> <div class=\"fnode\">I/O devices</div> </div>\n<p>Main characteristics:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Instructions and data are stored in <strong>the same memory</strong>.</li>\n<li>Memory is <strong>addressable by location</strong>; execution is <strong>sequential</strong> (PC-based).</li>\n<li>CPU has: <strong>PC</strong> (program counter), <strong>IR</strong> (instruction register), <strong>MAR</strong> (memory address register), <strong>MBR/MDR</strong> (memory buffer/data register), <strong>AC</strong> (accumulator).</li>\n<li>Data moves between CPU, memory and I/O over <strong>buses</strong> (data, address, control).</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><strong>Von Neumann bottleneck</strong> — because instructions and data share one bus and one memory, the CPU often waits for the memory: the single \"instruction &amp; data stream\" limits throughput. Harvard architecture (separate instruction and data memories) avoids it. Modern CPUs use caches and separate pipelines to soften the problem.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"evolution-and-computer-generations\">Evolution and Computer Generations<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#evolution-and-computer-generations\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Generation</th><th>Technology</th><th>Main feature</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First (1940s–50s)</td><td>Vacuum tubes</td><td>machine language, punched cards</td><td>ENIAC, UNIVAC</td></tr><tr><td>Second (1950s–60s)</td><td>Transistors</td><td>assembly language, magnetic core memory</td><td>IBM 7090</td></tr><tr><td>Third (1960s–70s)</td><td>ICs</td><td>high-level languages, operating systems</td><td>IBM 360, PDP-8</td></tr><tr><td>Fourth (1970s–90s)</td><td>LSI / VLSI microprocessors</td><td>microcomputer revolution, PCs</td><td>Intel 8086, 80386</td></tr><tr><td>Fifth (1990s–)</td><td>ULSI / parallel &amp; AI</td><td>parallel processing, multicore, AI</td><td>modern multicores, GPUs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"number-systems-recap\">Number Systems Recap<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#number-systems-recap\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Base</th><th>Digits</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Binary</td><td>2</td><td>0, 1</td><td><code>1010₂ = 10</code></td></tr><tr><td>Octal</td><td>8</td><td>0–7</td><td><code>12₈ = 10</code></td></tr><tr><td>Decimal</td><td>10</td><td>0–9</td><td><code>10</code></td></tr><tr><td>Hexadecimal</td><td>16</td><td>0–9, A–F</td><td><code>A16 = 161</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Conversions you must be fluent in:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Decimal → Binary : divide by 2, collect remainders bottom-to-top.\n  25 → 11001\nBinary → Decimal : multiply each bit by its position weight.\n  11001 → 16+8+0+0+1 = 25\nBinary → Hex     : group 4 bits from the right.\n  11001 → 0001 1001 → 19₁₆\nHex → Binary     : expand each hex digit to 4 bits.\n  A3 → 1010 0011</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"fixed-point-representation\">Fixed Point Representation<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#fixed-point-representation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A fixed-point number has a <strong>fixed</strong> number of bits for the integer part and a <strong>fixed</strong> number for the fractional part (the binary point position is fixed by convention).</p>\n<p>Example — Q4.4 format (4 integer bits + 4 fractional bits): the value <code>0011.1010</code> = 2 + 1 + 0.5 + 0.125 = <strong>3.625</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"representing-negative-integers\">Representing negative integers<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#representing-negative-integers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Idea</th><th>Example for −5 (8-bit)</th><th>Drawback</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sign-magnitude</td><td>1 sign bit + magnitude</td><td><code>10000101</code></td><td>two zeros (+0, −0)</td></tr><tr><td>1's complement</td><td>invert all bits</td><td><code>11111010</code></td><td>two zeros, end-around carry</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2's complement</strong></td><td>invert + add 1</td><td><code>11111011</code></td><td>one zero; standard in hardware</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>2's complement is used everywhere because <strong>addition and subtraction share one circuit</strong> (subtraction = add the 2's complement).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\"> 5  = 00000101\n−5  = invert → 11111010, +1 → 11111011\n 5 + (−5) = 00000101 + 11111011 = 1 00000000 → 0 (carry out is discarded)</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Overflow</strong> happens when the result of an operation exceeds the representable range. Detect it by comparing the <strong>carry into the sign bit</strong> and the <strong>carry out of the sign bit</strong> — if they differ, overflow.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">120 + 120 in 8-bit signed (max 127):\n  01111000 + 01111000 = 11110000 → looks negative → OVERFLOW</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"floating-point-representation-ieee-754\">Floating Point Representation (IEEE 754)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#floating-point-representation-ieee-754\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Floating point stores a number in normalized scientific form: <strong>±1.mantissa × 2<sup>exponent</sup></strong>, using three fields:</p>\n<div class=\"flow\"> <div class=\"fnode fmain\">Single precision (32 bits)</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Sign (1 bit) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Exponent (8 bits, biased by 127) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Mantissa/Fraction (23 bits)</div> <div class=\"fnode fmain\">Double precision (64 bits)</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Sign (1 bit) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Exponent (11 bits, biased by 1023) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Mantissa/Fraction (52 bits)</div> </div>\n<ul>\n<li>The leading <code>1.</code> of the mantissa is <strong>implicit</strong> (the hidden bit) — it is not stored.</li>\n<li>Exponent is stored <strong>biased</strong> (excess-127) so it can be compared like an unsigned number.</li>\n<li>Special values: exponent all 0s → zero/denormal; all 1s with mantissa 0 → ±infinity; all 1s with mantissa ≠ 0 → NaN.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Convert <strong>4.5</strong> to single precision. <code>4.5 = 100.1₂ = 1.001 × 2²</code> → sign 0, exponent = 2 + 127 = 129 = <code>10000001</code>, fraction = <code>0010...0</code> (23 bits). Result: <code>0 10000001 00100000000000000000000</code> = <code>0x40900000</code>.</p></div></aside>\n<p>Ranges (binary32): ≈ ±3.4 × 10<sup>38</sup>, smallest normal ≈ 1.2 × 10<sup>−38</sup>, ~7 significant decimal digits.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Floating point is <strong>approximate</strong>: 0.1 cannot be represented exactly in binary, so many decimal fractions round. This is why money should be kept as integers (cents/paise) in business software.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"decimal-arithmetic-bcd\">Decimal Arithmetic (BCD)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#decimal-arithmetic-bcd\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">BCD (Binary Coded Decimal)</mark> stores each decimal digit in 4 bits — <code>25</code> becomes <code>0010 0101</code>. It is used in calculators and financial systems because conversion to/from decimal is trivial and exact.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unpacked BCD</strong>: one digit per byte.</li>\n<li><strong>Packed BCD</strong>: two digits per byte.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>BCD addition</strong>: add the 4-bit groups; if the result is > 9 (or a carry occurs), add <strong>6 (0110)</strong> to correct it.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>15 + 27 in BCD: <code>0001 0101</code> + <code>0010 0111</code> = <code>0011 1100</code>; low nibble <code>1100</code> = 12 &gt; 9 so add 6: <code>1100 + 0110 = 0001 0010</code> (carry 1 into the next digit) → <code>0100 0010</code> = <strong>42</strong>. ✔</p></div></aside>\n<div class=\"flow\"> <div class=\"fnode\">Start — add BCD digits (4-bit each)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↓</div> <div class=\"fdiamond\">Sum > 9 or carry? (0000–1001)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">→ Yes ↓</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Add 0110 (6) correction, propagate carry</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↓</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Valid BCD result</div> </div>\n<h2 id=\"binary-addition-and-subtraction\">Binary Addition and Subtraction<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#binary-addition-and-subtraction\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><strong>Addition</strong> follows the normal rules (0+0=0, 0+1=1, 1+1=10 with carry). <strong>Subtraction</strong> in 2's complement: take the 2's complement of the subtrahend and <strong>add</strong> it.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>9 − 5: 5 = <code>00000101</code>, −5 = <code>11111011</code>; <code>00001001 + 11111011 = 1 00000100</code> → ignore carry → <strong>4</strong>. ✔</p></div></aside>\n<p><strong>Overflow rule for 2's complement addition:</strong> with operands of the <em>same sign</em> the result must have that sign; if A and B are positive and the sum is negative (or vice-versa), overflow has occurred.</p>\n<h2 id=\"multiplication-booth-s-algorithm\">Multiplication — Booth's Algorithm<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#multiplication-booth-s-algorithm\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Any binary multiplier can repeatedly do: <em>shift-add</em> (like long multiplication). For n-bit × n-bit this needs n additions — slow. <strong>Booth's algorithm</strong> is a signed multiplication method that <strong>skips runs of 1s</strong> in the multiplier using the idea of <em>recoding</em>: <code>0111...1</code> blocks are replaced by one subtraction and one addition.</p>\n<h3 id=\"booth-s-recoding-idea\">Booth's recoding idea<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#booth-s-recoding-idea\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>For a run of k ones starting at position i: <code>+2^i + 2^(i+1) + … + 2^(i+k−1) = 2^(i+k) − 2^i</code>. So a run of 1s needs only <strong>one subtraction and one addition</strong> instead of k additions.</p>\n<p>The algorithm inspects two bits of the multiplier at a time:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair (Q0 Q-1)</th><th>Operation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>00</td><td>no operation (shift)</td></tr><tr><td>01</td><td><strong>A = A + M</strong></td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><strong>A = A − M</strong></td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>no operation (shift)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>After every step the <strong>combined (A, Q, Q-1)</strong> is <strong>arithmetically shifted right</strong> (MSB of A replicates), and the count decreases.</p>\n<h3 id=\"algorithm-flowchart\">Algorithm (flowchart)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#algorithm-flowchart\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"flow\"> <div class=\"fnode\">Start: A = 0, Q-1 = 0, count = n; M = multiplicand, Q = multiplier</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↓</div> <div class=\"fdiamond\">count = 0?</div> <div class=\"farrow\">No ↓</div> <div class=\"fdiamond\">Q0 Q-1 = ?</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↓ 01 → A=A+M &nbsp;·&nbsp; 10 → A=A−M &nbsp;·&nbsp; 00 or 11 → no-op</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Arithmetic right shift (A, Q, Q-1)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↓</div> <div class=\"fnode\">count = count − 1 → repeat</div> <div class=\"farrow\">← back to count test ↑</div> <div class=\"fnode fmain\">Done: product = A : Q</div> </div>\n<h3 id=\"hardware-implementation\">Hardware implementation<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#hardware-implementation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"hw\"> <div class=\"hw-row\"> <div class=\"hw-box hw-alu\">Adder / Subtractor (n-bit ALU)</div> <div class=\"hw-arrow\">↔</div> <div class=\"hw-box\">A register (n bits, starts 0)</div> </div> <div class=\"hw-row\"> <div class=\"hw-box\">M register — multiplicand</div> <div class=\"hw-arrow\">→</div> <div class=\"hw-box hw-alu\">ALU</div> <div class=\"hw-arrow\">→</div> <div class=\"hw-box\">A register</div> </div> <div class=\"hw-row\"> <div class=\"hw-box\">Q register — multiplier (n bits)</div> <div class=\"hw-arrow\">→</div> <div class=\"hw-box hw-small\">Q-1 flip-flop</div> <div class=\"hw-note\">Control logic watches Q0 Q-1, drives Add / Subtract / Arithmetic shift; A and Q shift together (A·Q holds the product).</div> </div> </div>\n<h3 id=\"worked-example-7-3-21\">Worked example: 7 × 3 = 21<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#worked-example-7-3-21\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>M = 0111 (7), Q = 0011 (3), A = 0000, Q-1 = 0, count = 4.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Count</th><th>Q0 Q-1</th><th>Operation</th><th>A</th><th>Q</th><th>Q-1</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4</td><td>10</td><td>A = A − M = 0000 − 0111 = 1001</td><td>1001</td><td>0011</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>—</td><td>shift right</td><td>1100</td><td>1001</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>11</td><td>no-op (shift)</td><td>1110</td><td>0100</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>01</td><td>A = A + M = 1110 + 0111 = 0101</td><td>0101</td><td>0100</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>—</td><td>shift right → done</td><td>0010</td><td>1010</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>—</td><td>shift right (final)</td><td><strong>0001</strong></td><td><strong>0101</strong></td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Result <code>A:Q = 0001 0101</code> = <strong>21</strong> ✔</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Note</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Booth's algorithm works with <strong>signed</strong> (2's complement) numbers directly and speeds up multiplication when the multiplier contains long runs of 1s. The worst case (alternating bits like <code>1010…</code>) needs the same number of operations as plain shift-and-add.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"multiprocessors-and-multicomputers\">Multiprocessors and Multicomputers<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#multiprocessors-and-multicomputers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Multiprocessor</th><th>Multicomputer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CPUs</td><td>multiple CPUs share <strong>one memory</strong> (tightly coupled)</td><td>each computer has <strong>its own memory</strong> (loosely coupled)</td></tr><tr><td>Communication</td><td>shared memory / bus</td><td>message passing over a network</td></tr><tr><td>Failure</td><td>one CPU failure affects the whole system</td><td>more fault tolerant</td></tr><tr><td>Programming</td><td>parallel programming (threads, shared variables)</td><td>distributed programming (send/receive)</td></tr><tr><td>Also called</td><td>shared-memory system</td><td>cluster</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Flynn's taxonomy</strong> classifies machines by instruction stream × data stream:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SISD</td><td>single instruction, single data</td><td>ordinary single-core PC</td></tr><tr><td>SIMD</td><td>single instruction, multiple data</td><td>GPU vector units</td></tr><tr><td>MISD</td><td>multiple instructions, single data</td><td>rare (fault-tolerant designs)</td></tr><tr><td>MIMD</td><td>multiple instructions, multiple data</td><td>multiprocessors, multicomputers</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"performance-measures-mips-and-mflops\">Performance Measures — MIPS and MFLOPS<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#performance-measures-mips-and-mflops\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CPI</strong> (cycles per instruction) = average clock cycles per instruction.</li>\n<li><strong>CPU time</strong> = instruction count × CPI × clock cycle time.</li>\n<li><mark class=\"kw\">MIPS</mark> = millions of instructions executed per second = <code>clock_rate / (CPI × 10⁶)</code>.</li>\n<li><mark class=\"kw\">MFLOPS</mark> = millions of floating-point operations per second = <code>floating point ops / (execution time × 10⁶)</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A 2 GHz (2 × 10⁹ Hz) processor at CPI = 2 executes: MIPS = 2 × 10⁹ / (2 × 10⁶) = <strong>1000 MIPS</strong>. If it performs 6 × 10⁷ floating point ops in 0.05 s → MFLOPS = 6 × 10⁷ / (0.05 × 10⁶) = <strong>1200 MFLOPS</strong>.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>MIPS is a <strong>questionable</strong> metric: a machine can achieve high MIPS on easy instructions but take longer on real programs, and MIPS varies between ISAs. MFLOPS is fairer for scientific workloads. The reliable measure is the <strong>execution time</strong> of a benchmark.</p></div></aside>\n<p><strong>Amdahl's law</strong> (full treatment in Unit 2) warns that the speed-up of a whole system is limited by the fraction of work that <strong>cannot</strong> be sped up.</p>\n<h2 id=\"memory-organization\">Memory Organization<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#memory-organization\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"memory-hierarchy\">Memory hierarchy<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#memory-hierarchy\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The hierarchy exploits the principle of <strong>locality</strong> (programs reuse recent data/instructions). Fast, expensive memory is placed closest to the CPU; slow, cheap, huge storage at the bottom.</p>\n<div class=\"flow\"> <div class=\"fnode fmain\">Registers (CPU internal, ≈ 1 ns, few hundred bytes)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↑ faster · smaller · costlier per byte ↓</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Cache L1 / L2 / L3 (SRAM, a few MB)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↕</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Main memory (DRAM, GBs)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↕</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Secondary storage (SSD / HDD — TBs)</div> </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Technology</th><th>Typical size</th><th>Access time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Register</td><td>flip-flops</td><td>100s B</td><td>~1 ns</td></tr><tr><td>Cache</td><td>SRAM</td><td>KB–MB</td><td>1–10 ns</td></tr><tr><td>Main memory</td><td>DRAM</td><td>GB</td><td>~100 ns</td></tr><tr><td>Disk / SSD</td><td>magnetic / flash</td><td>TB</td><td>ms</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"types-of-memory\">Types of memory<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#types-of-memory\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Volatile?</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>SRAM</strong></td><td>yes</td><td>cache (fast, 6 transistors/bit)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DRAM</strong></td><td>yes (needs refresh)</td><td>main memory (1 transistor + capacitor/bit)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ROM</strong></td><td>no</td><td>firmware (programmed once)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PROM</strong></td><td>no</td><td>one-time programmable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>EPROM</strong></td><td>no</td><td>erasable with UV light</td></tr><tr><td><strong>EEPROM / Flash</strong></td><td>no</td><td>electrically erasable; USB drives, SSDs, BIOS</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"tlb-translation-lookaside-buffer\">TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tlb-translation-lookaside-buffer\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">TLB</mark> is a small, very fast <strong>cache of page-table entries</strong>. When the CPU produces a <strong>virtual address</strong>, the MMU must translate it to a <strong>physical address</strong> using the page table (kept in main memory). Walking the page table on every access is slow, so recent translations are cached in the TLB.</p>\n<div class=\"flow\"> <div class=\"fnode\">CPU issues virtual address</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↓</div> <div class=\"fdiamond\">TLB hit? (tag = page number)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">→ Hit ↓</div> <div class=\"fnode fmain\">Frame number obtained instantly → physical address → cache/memory</div> <div class=\"farrow\">← Miss (else) ↓</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Walk the page table in memory → load entry into TLB</div> </div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>TLB hit</strong> — translation is fast (no memory access for the page table).</li>\n<li><strong>TLB miss</strong> — the MMU must access the page table (extra memory accesses) or raise a <strong>page fault</strong> if the page is not in memory.</li>\n<li>Like caches, the TLB uses <strong>associative or set-associative</strong> lookup and <strong>LRU</strong> replacement.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Remember the chain: <strong>Virtual address → TLB (translation cache) → page table (in memory) → physical address → cache → main memory.</strong> TLB speeds up address translation; cache speeds up data access — they are different caches at different levels.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"quick-revision\">Quick Revision<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-revision\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>One-line answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Organization vs architecture</td><td>how it's built vs what it offers</td></tr><tr><td>Von Neumann</td><td>program + data in one memory, sequential fetch-execute</td></tr><tr><td>2's complement</td><td>invert + add 1; single add/sub circuit</td></tr><tr><td>IEEE 754</td><td>±1.m × 2<sup>e</sup>, biased exponent, hidden bit</td></tr><tr><td>BCD correction</td><td>add 6 when a nibble sum > 9</td></tr><tr><td>Booth's algorithm</td><td>signed multiply; recodes runs of 1s; uses 00/01/10/11 pairs</td></tr><tr><td>MIPS / MFLOPS</td><td>performance rates based on instructions / float ops</td></tr><tr><td>Memory hierarchy</td><td>registers → cache → RAM → disk (faster, smaller, costlier upward)</td></tr><tr><td>TLB</td><td>cache of page table entries for address translation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice Questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#practice-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Distinguish computer organization from computer architecture with examples.</li>\n<li>Explain the Von Neumann architecture with a block diagram. What is the Von Neumann bottleneck?</li>\n<li>Represent −13 in 8-bit sign-magnitude, 1's complement and 2's complement. Add 13 and −13 in 2's complement.</li>\n<li>Convert 6.75 to IEEE 754 single precision.</li>\n<li>Add 28 + 19 in BCD and show the correction step.</li>\n<li>Multiply (−6) × 5 using Booth's algorithm. Show all steps.</li>\n<li>Draw the flowchart of Booth's algorithm and label its hardware registers.</li>\n<li>Explain Flynn's taxonomy. Differentiate a multiprocessor from a multicomputer.</li>\n<li>Compute MIPS for a 2.4 GHz machine with CPI = 3.</li>\n<li>Draw the memory hierarchy. Explain why TLB is needed and what happens on a TLB miss.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"id": "coa-u2", "title": "COA Unit 2 — CPU Design, Control Unit, Microprogramming, CISC & RISC", "subject": "coa", "unit": 2, "description": "General register and stack organization, the common bus system, computer instructions, timing and control, I/O and the interrupt cycle, Amdahl's law, microprogrammed control — control memory, address sequencing, microinstruction formats — plus hardwired vs microprogrammed control and CISC vs RISC.", "tags": "registers, stack, bus, interrupts, amdahl, microprogrammed control, cisc, risc", "date": "2026-02-14", "featured": 1, "words": 2001, "reading_min": 10, "views": 264, "body_html": "<h2 id=\"general-register-organization\">General Register Organization<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#general-register-organization\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The <mark class=\"kw\">general register organization</mark> is the CPU design where several registers feed into an <strong>ALU</strong> through multiplexers, and the <strong>control unit</strong> supplies a <em>control word</em> that selects which two operands the ALU processes and where the result goes.</p>\n<div class=\"hw\"> <div class=\"hw-row\"><div class=\"hw-box hw-alu\">ALU (arithmetic-logic)</div></div> <div class=\"hw-row\"> <div class=\"hw-box\">R0</div><div class=\"hw-box\">R1</div><div class=\"hw-box\">…</div><div class=\"hw-box\">R(n−1)</div><div class=\"hw-box\">MUX A</div><div class=\"hw-box\">MUX B</div> </div> <div class=\"hw-row\"><div class=\"hw-box\">Control word: SELA (select A) · SELB (select B) · OPR (operation) · SELD (destination)</div></div> </div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SELA / SELB</strong> — choose the two source registers (multiplexer select bits).</li>\n<li><strong>OPR</strong> — select the ALU operation (add, subtract, AND, OR, XOR, shift…).</li>\n<li><strong>SELD</strong> — choose the destination register for the result.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>control word</strong> for a 3-operand instruction <code>R3 ← R1 + R2</code> would be: <code>SELA=001</code> (R1), <code>SELB=010</code> (R2), <code>OPR=ADD</code>, <code>SELD=011</code> (R3). The control unit decodes each field and enables the right data paths; <strong>hardwired and microprogrammed</strong> control units differ only in <em>how</em> these control words are generated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"stack-organization\">Stack Organization<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#stack-organization\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">stack</mark> is a <strong>Last-In-First-Out (LIFO)</strong> structure with two operations:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>PUSH</strong></td><td>place an item on top</td></tr><tr><td><strong>POP</strong></td><td>remove the top item (and read it)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"register-stack\">Register stack<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#register-stack\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A stack of registers with a <code>SP</code> (stack pointer) that always points to the top item.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">PUSH: SP ← SP + 1 ;  M[SP] ← data      (if SP == FULL → stack-full flag)\nPOP : data ← M[SP]  ;  SP ← SP − 1     (if SP == 0 → stack-empty flag)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Eight registers: <code>FULL</code> when SP = 7, <code>EMPTY</code> when SP = 0 (common implementation uses \"full descending\" stacks).</p>\n<h3 id=\"memory-stack\">Memory stack<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#memory-stack\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The same logic with a memory address range for the stack; <code>SP</code> holds the address of the top element. Memory is <em>not</em> cleared on push/pop — a new push simply overwrites the old top.</p>\n<h3 id=\"uses-of-the-stack\">Uses of the stack<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#uses-of-the-stack\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Subroutine call/return (PUSH return address, POP to restore PC).</li>\n<li>Expression evaluation (postfix — see Data Structures notes).</li>\n<li>Reversing data, matching brackets, and handling interrupts.</li>\n<li>Function parameters and local variables (call frame) in high-level compilers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"common-bus-system\">Common Bus System<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#common-bus-system\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">bus</mark> is a set of common lines that transfer data between registers. To avoid dedicated wires between every pair of registers, all registers connect to a <strong>common bus</strong> through <strong>three-state buffers</strong>; the selection lines enable exactly <strong>one</strong> source at a time.</p>\n<div class=\"hw\"> <div class=\"hw-row\">Common bus (8/16/32 lines) — only one source enabled at a time</div> <div class=\"hw-row\"> <div class=\"hw-box\">R0</div><div class=\"hw-box\">R1</div><div class=\"hw-box\">R2</div><div class=\"hw-box\">R3</div><div class=\"hw-box\">PC</div><div class=\"hw-box\">AR</div><div class=\"hw-box\">M[AR]→DR</div><div class=\"hw-box\">INPR</div> </div> <div class=\"hw-row\"><div class=\"hw-box\">Select lines S2 S1 S0 → select which register's 3-state buffers drive the bus</div></div> </div>\n<ul>\n<li>Each register's output passes through <strong>3-state buffers</strong> (high impedance = disconnected).</li>\n<li>A 3-to-8 decoder decodes the <strong>selection code</strong> (e.g. <code>S2S1S0 = 101</code> selects AR) and enables that register's buffers.</li>\n<li>The <strong>destination</strong> register receives bus data when its <strong>load (LD)</strong> input is pulsed by the control unit.</li>\n<li>Memory is handled the same way: <code>M[AR]</code> can be placed on the bus, and <code>DR</code> can load from the bus — this is exactly how the Basic Computer of Mano moves data.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>To copy PC into AR: set selection lines to the PC code, pulse the <code>LD</code> (load) input of AR. One bus transfer, one clock pulse. Register-to-register transfers between any two registers need just select + load.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"computer-instructions\">Computer Instructions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#computer-instructions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>An <mark class=\"kw\">instruction</mark> is a command the CPU understands; it usually contains an <strong>opcode</strong> (what to do) and <strong>operands/addresses</strong> (where the data is).</p>\n<h3 id=\"instruction-formats-basic-computer-16-bit\">Instruction formats (Basic Computer, 16-bit)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#instruction-formats-basic-computer-16-bit\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Bits</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Memory-reference</td><td>15–12 opcode, 11–0 address</td><td>operand in memory</td></tr><tr><td>Register-reference</td><td>15 = 0/opcode 111, rest = operation</td><td>operate on AC</td></tr><tr><td>Input-Output</td><td>15–12 = 1111, rest = I/O command</td><td>INPR/OUTR control</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"common-instruction-types\">Common instruction types<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#common-instruction-types\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Data transfer:</strong> <code>LOAD</code>, <code>STORE</code>, <code>MOV</code>, <code>PUSH</code>, <code>POP</code></li>\n<li><strong>Arithmetic:</strong> <code>ADD</code>, <code>SUB</code>, <code>MUL</code>, <code>DIV</code>, <code>INC</code>, <code>DEC</code></li>\n<li><strong>Logical:</strong> <code>AND</code>, <code>OR</code>, <code>XOR</code>, <code>NOT</code>, shifts</li>\n<li><strong>Control transfer:</strong> <code>JUMP</code>, <code>CALL</code>, <code>RETURN</code>, <code>BRANCH</code> (conditional)</li>\n<li><strong>I/O:</strong> <code>IN</code>, <code>OUT</code></li>\n<li><strong>Program control:</strong> <code>HALT</code>, <code>NOP</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"timing-and-control\">Timing and Control<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#timing-and-control\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The CPU must sequence every microoperation with clock pulses. A <strong>sequence counter (SC)</strong> and <strong>decoder</strong> generate the timing signals <code>T0, T1, T2, …</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"fetch-cycle-basic-computer\">Fetch cycle (basic computer)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#fetch-cycle-basic-computer\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Timing</th><th>Micro-operation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>T0</code></td><td>AR ← PC (put program counter on address bus)</td></tr><tr><td><code>T1</code></td><td>IR ← M[AR] ; PC ← PC + 1 (read instruction, increment PC)</td></tr><tr><td><code>T2</code></td><td>Decode IR; D0…D7 = opcode decoder; if indirect bit, fetch operand address</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Then the <strong>execute</strong> cycle for that instruction (e.g. <code>ADD</code>): DR ← M[AR]; AC ← AC + DR.</p>\n<p><strong>Instruction cycle = fetch → (indirect) → execute → (interrupt).</strong></p>\n<p>Two ways to build the control unit:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Hardwired control</th><th>Microprogrammed control</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Implementation</td><td>logic gates + flip-flops</td><td>control memory (ROM)</td></tr><tr><td>Changeability</td><td>fixed at design time</td><td>change microinstructions, not hardware</td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>fast</td><td>slower (ROM access per microstep)</td></tr><tr><td>Cost for complex ISAs</td><td>high, error-prone</td><td>low, structured</td></tr><tr><td>Used in</td><td>RISC, simple CPUs</td><td>complex ISAs historically (CISC)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"input-output-and-interrupts\">Input, Output and Interrupts<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#input-output-and-interrupts\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Programmed I/O</strong> — the CPU polls/wait-loops for an I/O device; simple but wastes CPU time.</li>\n<li><strong>Interrupt I/O</strong> — the device <em>signals</em> the CPU when ready; the CPU finishes the current instruction and services the device.</li>\n<li><strong>DMA</strong> — a controller moves blocks of data directly to/from memory without CPU involvement per byte.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"types-of-interrupts\">Types of interrupts<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#types-of-interrupts\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Internal (software/trap)</td><td>division by zero, system calls, <code>INT</code> instruction</td></tr><tr><td>External (hardware)</td><td>device requesting service, timer, power failure</td></tr><tr><td>Maskable</td><td>can be disabled by software</td></tr><tr><td>Non-maskable (NMI)</td><td>must always be serviced (e.g. power loss)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-interrupt-cycle-mano-basic-computer\">The interrupt cycle (Mano Basic Computer)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#the-interrupt-cycle-mano-basic-computer\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>When the CPU is enabled for interrupts (<code>IEN=1</code>) and an interrupt arrives (<code>R</code> flip-flop set):</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Timing</th><th>Micro-operation</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>T0</code></td><td>AR ← 0 ; TR ← PC</td><td>prepare memory location 0; save return address</td></tr><tr><td><code>T1</code></td><td>M[0] ← TR ; PC ← 0</td><td>store return address at address 0</td></tr><tr><td><code>T2</code></td><td>PC ← PC + 1 ; IEN ← 0 ; R ← 0</td><td>jump to address 1; disable further interrupts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The interrupt service routine starts at address <strong>1</strong> (which usually contains a <code>JUMP</code> to the real handler). At the end, the handler restores the return address and re-enables interrupts.</p>\n<div class=\"flow\"> <div class=\"fnode\">Instruction cycle ends (execute done)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↓</div> <div class=\"fdiamond\">Interrupt? (IEN=1 and R=1)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">No → next fetch</div> <div class=\"farrow\">Yes ↓</div> <div class=\"fnode\">T0: AR←0, TR←PC &nbsp;→&nbsp; T1: M[0]←TR, PC←0 &nbsp;→&nbsp; T2: PC←PC+1, IEN←0, R←0</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↓</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Execute interrupt service routine at address 1 → return to saved address</div> </div>\n<h2 id=\"design-drivers\">Design Drivers<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#design-drivers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"design-for-the-common-case\">Design for the common case<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#design-for-the-common-case\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Hardware should favor the <strong>most frequent</strong> operation. Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Make the <strong>register-register</strong> path fastest (most instructions use registers).</li>\n<li><strong>Instruction prefetch</strong> and <strong>pipelined fetch</strong> because fetches happen every cycle.</li>\n<li>Fast <strong>cache</strong> on the memory the CPU touches most (locality).</li>\n<li>In x86, the most common instruction lengths get short encodings.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"amdahl-s-law\">Amdahl's law<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#amdahl-s-law\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The overall speed-up is limited by the fraction <code>f</code> of the task that is improved, no matter how fast you make the rest:</p>\n<blockquote><p>Speedup = 1 / [(1 − f) + f / s]   where s = speed-up of the improved part.</p></blockquote>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>40% of a program (f = 0.4) becomes 5× faster (s = 5): Speedup = 1 / [0.6 + 0.4/5] = 1 / 0.68 = <strong>1.47×</strong>. Even with s → ∞ you only reach 1/0.6 = 1.67× — the <strong>unimproved 60%</strong> dominates.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Amdahl's law is the classic exam trap: never quote just the \"5×\" — quote the small <strong>bound</strong> (1.67×) and the actual 1.47×.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"microprogrammed-control-organization\">Microprogrammed Control Organization<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#microprogrammed-control-organization\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <strong>microprogrammed control unit</strong> implements the control logic as a <strong>microprogram</strong> stored in <mark class=\"kw\">control memory</mark> (a fast ROM). Each microinstruction controls one clock step.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Microinstruction</strong> — one row of control memory; sets control signals for one step.</li>\n<li><strong>Microoperation</strong> — an elementary operation (register transfer) caused by control bits.</li>\n<li><strong>Microprogram</strong> — the sequence of microinstructions that implements a machine instruction.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"hw\"> <div class=\"hw-row\"><div class=\"hw-box hw-alu\">Control memory (ROM) — microinstructions</div></div> <div class=\"hw-row\"><div class=\"hw-box\">Next-address generator + incrementer + branch logic</div><div class=\"hw-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"hw-box\">Control address register (CAR)</div><div class=\"hw-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"hw-box\">Control memory</div><div class=\"hw-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"hw-box\">MIR — microinstruction register → control signals</div></div> </div>\n<h3 id=\"microinstruction-format\">Microinstruction format<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#microinstruction-format\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Each microinstruction usually has three functional groups: <strong>control bits</strong> (micro-operations to perform), <strong>condition select (CD)</strong>, <strong>branch control (BR)</strong> and the <strong>next address (AD)</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"fields\"> <span class=\"field f1\">F1 (op 1)</span><span class=\"field f1\">F2 (op 2)</span><span class=\"field f1\">F3 (op 3)</span><span class=\"field cd\">CD (condition)</span><span class=\"field br\">BR (branch)</span><span class=\"field ad\">AD (address)</span> </div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>F1…F3</strong> — select micro-operations (encoded; decoded into control signals).</li>\n<li><strong>CD</strong> — which status bit is tested (I, S, Z, or none).</li>\n<li><strong>BR</strong> — branch type (JMP, CALL, RET, MAP, none).</li>\n<li><strong>AD</strong> — branch/next address.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"address-sequencing\">Address sequencing<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#address-sequencing\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The next microinstruction address comes from one of four sources:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Incrementer</strong> — next sequential microinstruction (<code>CAR + 1</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Mapping logic</strong> (MAP) — opcode of the machine instruction selects the start of its microprogram.</li>\n<li><strong>Branch/subroutine logic</strong> — conditional branch taken from <code>AD</code>; subroutine calls/returns via a <strong>return address stack</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Reset</strong> — first microinstruction (address 0).</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"flow\"> <div class=\"fnode\">Machine instruction → opcode → mapping ROM gives microaddress</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↓</div> <div class=\"fdiamond\">Conditional branch? (CD bit)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">No ↓</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Next = CAR + 1 (incrementer)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">Yes ↓</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Next = AD from microinstruction (or subroutine call/return via stack)</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↓</div> <div class=\"fnode fmain\">Load CAR → fetch microinstruction → execute → repeat</div> </div>\n<h3 id=\"horizontal-vs-vertical-microprogramming\">Horizontal vs Vertical microprogramming<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#horizontal-vs-vertical-microprogramming\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><strong>Horizontal</strong></th><th><strong>Vertical</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Control bits</td><td>many bits, one per control signal (wide)</td><td>few bits, encoded (narrow)</td></tr><tr><td>Parallelism</td><td>many micro-ops per instruction</td><td>one micro-op per instruction</td></tr><tr><td>Decoding</td><td>little-to-none</td><td>heavy (decoder needed)</td></tr><tr><td>Word size</td><td>long</td><td>short</td></tr><tr><td>ROM size / speed</td><td>large but fast</td><td>smaller but slower per microstep</td></tr><tr><td>Flexibility</td><td>high</td><td>lower</td></tr><tr><td>Contrast</td><td>like RISC thinking</td><td>like CISC thinking</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"microprogram-sequencer\">Microprogram sequencer<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#microprogram-sequencer\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A <strong>microprogram sequencer</strong> is the hardware that generates the next address:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Sources: incrementer / branch address / mapping ROM / subroutine stack\n     |\n     v\n MUX (selected by branch logic) → CAR → control memory → MIR → control signals</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"hardwired-vs-microprogrammed-control\">Hardwired vs Microprogrammed Control<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#hardwired-vs-microprogrammed-control\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Hardwired</th><th>Microprogrammed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nature</td><td>sequential logic circuit</td><td>program in control memory</td></tr><tr><td>Design</td><td>difficult, unstructured</td><td>simple, regular</td></tr><tr><td>Modify</td><td>redesign hardware</td><td>update microprogram</td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>fast</td><td>slower (memory read per step)</td></tr><tr><td>Cost (complex ISAs)</td><td>high</td><td>low</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>RISC, fixed simple CPUs</td><td>complex/CISC instruction sets</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"cisc-and-risc\">CISC and RISC<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cisc-and-risc\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"cisc-complex-instruction-set-computer\">CISC — Complex Instruction Set Computer<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cisc-complex-instruction-set-computer\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Many complex instructions</strong>, some spanning multiple clock cycles.</li>\n<li><strong>Variable-length instructions</strong>, fewer instructions per program.</li>\n<li>Rich addressing modes; instructions can operate on <strong>memory directly</strong>.</li>\n<li>Hardware (microprogram) does the heavy lifting — smaller code size.</li>\n<li>Examples: Intel x86 family.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"risc-reduced-instruction-set-computer\">RISC — Reduced Instruction Set Computer<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#risc-reduced-instruction-set-computer\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Small, simple, uniform</strong> instruction set — most complete in <strong>one clock cycle</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fixed-length</strong> instructions; <strong>load-store</strong> architecture (only LOAD/STORE touch memory).</li>\n<li><strong>Many registers</strong> (~32); <strong>pipelining</strong> friendly; compiler does the optimization.</li>\n<li><strong>Hardwired control</strong> (fast); no microprogram for instructions.</li>\n<li>Examples: ARM, MIPS, RISC-V.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>CISC</th><th>RISC</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Instruction size</td><td>variable</td><td>fixed (32-bit)</td></tr><tr><td>Clock cycles/instruction</td><td>many</td><td>one (typically)</td></tr><tr><td>Addressing modes</td><td>many</td><td>few</td></tr><tr><td>Memory access</td><td>instructions may access memory</td><td>only load/store</td></tr><tr><td>Registers</td><td>fewer</td><td>many</td></tr><tr><td>Control</td><td>microprogrammed</td><td>hardwired</td></tr><tr><td>Pipelining</td><td>harder</td><td>easy</td></tr><tr><td>Code size</td><td>small</td><td>larger</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>x86, 68000</td><td>ARM, MIPS, RISC-V</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Exam favourite: \"Compare CISC and RISC.\" Anchor your answer on <strong>five axes</strong>: instruction complexity/size, cycles per instruction, where operations happen (memory vs registers), control unit type, and register count.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"quick-revision\">Quick Revision<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-revision\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>One-liner</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Control word</td><td>SELA · SELB · OPR · SELD selects ALU operands/destination</td></tr><tr><td>Stack</td><td>LIFO; PUSH increments SP, POP decrements</td></tr><tr><td>Common bus</td><td>shared lines + 3-state buffers, one source at a time</td></tr><tr><td>Interrupt cycle</td><td>save PC at M[0], jump to 1, disable further interrupts</td></tr><tr><td>Amdahl's law</td><td>Speedup = 1/[(1−f) + f/s]</td></tr><tr><td>Microinstruction</td><td>(F1,F2,F3,CD,BR,AD) one-step control row in ROM</td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal vs vertical</td><td>wide &amp; parallel vs narrow &amp; encoded</td></tr><tr><td>Hardwired vs micro</td><td>fast logic vs stored program</td></tr><tr><td>CISC vs RISC</td><td>rich complex vs simple load-store pipelined</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice Questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#practice-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Explain general register organization of a CPU with the control word fields.</li>\n<li>Write PUSH/POP microsequences for a register stack and a memory stack. List four uses of a stack.</li>\n<li>Draw the common bus system for 8 registers with three-state buffers and selection lines.</li>\n<li>Explain the interrupt cycle of the Basic Computer with the timing signals.</li>\n<li>State and prove-by-example Amdahl's law. When is the speed-up bound reached?</li>\n<li>Explain the microinstruction format and the four sources of next address.</li>\n<li>Compare horizontal and vertical microprogramming.</li>\n<li>Compare hardwired and microprogrammed control units.</li>\n<li>Compare CISC and RISC on at least six points.</li>\n<li>Draw the microprogram sequencer block diagram and trace one branch.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"id": "ds-u1", "title": "DS Unit 1 — Complexity, Recursion, Arrays & Searching/Sorting", "subject": "ds", "unit": 1, "description": "Introduction to data structures, built-in vs user-defined types, algorithm analysis with worst/best/average case, Big-O/Omega/Theta notation, recursion, 1D/2D arrays and sparse matrices, linear and binary search, and selection, insertion and bubble sort — with algorithms, charts and C++ examples.", "tags": "arrays, sparse matrix, linear search, binary search, bubble sort, selection sort, insertion sort, big-o", "date": "2026-02-09", "featured": 1, "words": 1907, "reading_min": 10, "views": 388, "body_html": "<h2 id=\"introduction-to-data-structures\">Introduction to Data Structures<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#introduction-to-data-structures\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">data structure</mark> is an organized way of storing and managing data so that operations (insert, delete, search, sort, update) are efficient. A good choice of data structure is often the difference between an O(n) and an O(log n) program.</p>\n<h3 id=\"data-types\">Data types<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#data-types\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Built-in / primitive</strong></td><td>supplied by the language</td><td><code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>bool</code>, pointer</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Derived</strong></td><td>built from primitives</td><td>arrays, strings, functions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>User-defined</strong></td><td>defined by the programmer</td><td><code>struct</code>, <code>class</code>, <code>union</code>, <code>enum</code>, linked structures</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"linear-vs-non-linear-structures\">Linear vs Non-linear structures<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#linear-vs-non-linear-structures\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Linear</th><th>Non-linear</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>elements in a sequence</td><td>elements in a hierarchy</td></tr><tr><td>one-to-one relation</td><td>one-to-many relation</td></tr><tr><td>arrays, linked lists, stacks, queues</td><td>trees, graphs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"applications-of-data-structures\">Applications of data structures<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#applications-of-data-structures\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arrays</strong> — matrices, lookup tables, buffers.</li>\n<li><strong>Stacks</strong> — expression evaluation, function calls (recursion), undo, backtracking.</li>\n<li><strong>Queues</strong> — scheduling (CPU, print spooling), BFS, buffers.</li>\n<li><strong>Linked lists</strong> — dynamic memory, symbol tables, polynomial arithmetic.</li>\n<li><strong>Trees</strong> — file systems, databases (B-trees), compilers (syntax trees).</li>\n<li><strong>Graphs</strong> — networks, maps/GPS, social graphs, circuit design.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"algorithm-analysis\">Algorithm Analysis<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#algorithm-analysis\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>An <mark class=\"kw\">algorithm</mark> is a finite sequence of well-defined steps to solve a problem. Analysis answers: <strong>how much time? how much space?</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"cases-of-analysis\">Cases of analysis<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cases-of-analysis\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>When</th><th>Example (binary search)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Best case</strong></td><td>data is favourable; fewest steps</td><td>key at the middle — O(1)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Worst case</strong></td><td>data is unfavourable; most steps</td><td>key missing — O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Average case</strong></td><td>expected over all inputs</td><td>O(log n)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Time complexity</strong> — number of basic operations as a function of input size <code>n</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Space complexity</strong> — memory used (input data + auxiliary space + stack) as a function of <code>n</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"asymptotic-notations\">Asymptotic notations<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#asymptotic-notations\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Notation</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Like \"bounds\"</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Big-O O(...)</strong></td><td>upper bound (worst case growth)</td><td>≤</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Omega Ω(...)</strong></td><td>lower bound (best case growth)</td><td>≥</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Theta Θ(...)</strong></td><td>tight bound (both)</td><td>=</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-def\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Definition</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>f(n) = O(g(n)) if ∃ c, n₀ such that <strong>0 ≤ f(n) ≤ c·g(n)</strong> for all n ≥ n₀ (\"f grows no faster than g\"). f(n) = Ω(g(n)) if <strong>f(n) ≥ c·g(n)</strong> for n ≥ n₀. f(n) = Θ(g(n)) if both hold.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"common-growth-rates-chart\">Common growth rates (chart)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#common-growth-rates-chart\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>n</th><th>log₂ n</th><th>n</th><th>n log₂ n</th><th>n²</th><th>2ⁿ</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10</td><td>3</td><td>10</td><td>33</td><td>100</td><td>1,024</td></tr><tr><td>100</td><td>7</td><td>100</td><td>664</td><td>10⁴</td><td>1.3×10³⁰</td></tr><tr><td>1,000</td><td>10</td><td>10³</td><td>9,966</td><td>10⁶</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>10⁶</td><td>20</td><td>10⁶</td><td>2×10⁷</td><td>10¹²</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Ordered from fastest to slowest:</p>\n<blockquote><p>O(1) &lt; O(log n) &lt; O(n) &lt; O(n log n) &lt; O(n²) &lt; O(n³) &lt; O(2ⁿ) &lt; O(n!)</p></blockquote>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Rule of thumb for counting steps: look at the <strong>innermost loop</strong> body. One loop over n → O(n); nested loops → O(n²); halving the search space each step → O(log n).</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"basics-of-recursion\">Basics of Recursion<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#basics-of-recursion\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Recursion</mark> occurs when a function calls <strong>itself</strong> on a smaller instance. Two mandatory parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Base case</strong> — stops the recursion (return without calling itself).</li>\n<li><strong>Recursive case</strong> — reduces the problem size and calls itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int sum(int n) {                 // sum of 1..n\n    if (n == 0) return 0;        // base case\n    return n + sum(n - 1);       // recursive case\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>system stack</strong> stores each pending call (activation record: parameters, locals, return address).</li>\n<li><strong>Stack overflow</strong> occurs when recursion is too deep (no base case / huge depth).</li>\n<li>Every recursive program can be rewritten iteratively (using an explicit stack), and vice versa.</li>\n<li><strong>Tail recursion</strong> (recursive call is the last statement) can be optimized into a loop by the compiler.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><code>sum(4) = 4 + sum(3) = 4 + 3 + sum(2) = 4 + 3 + 2 + sum(1) = 4+3+2+1+0 = 10</code>. Each call is pushed, then popped back in reverse.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"arrays\">Arrays<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#arrays\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>An <mark class=\"kw\">array</mark> is a collection of <strong>homogeneous</strong> elements stored in <strong>contiguous memory</strong>, accessed by an <strong>index</strong>. Elements are at fixed, computable addresses, so access is O(1).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};   // 1-D array\n// base address of a = &amp;a[0];  a[i] is at base + i * sizeof(int)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"one-dimensional-arrays\">One-dimensional arrays<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#one-dimensional-arrays\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Formula: <code>address(a[i]) = base + i × size</code></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>access a[i]</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td>insert at position i (shifting)</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>delete at position i</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>linear search</td><td>O(n)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"two-dimensional-arrays\">Two-dimensional arrays<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#two-dimensional-arrays\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>int a[R][C]</code> has R rows and C columns. Memory is laid out either way:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Layout</th><th>Address of a[i][j]</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Row-major</strong> (C, C++, Java)</td><td><code>base + (i × C + j) × size</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Column-major</strong> (Fortran)</td><td><code>base + (j × R + i) × size</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int marks[3][2] = { {71, 82}, {66, 90}, {88, 79} };\n// marks[2][1]  (row-major): base + (2*2 + 1) * sizeof(int)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"multi-dimensional-arrays\">Multi-dimensional arrays<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#multi-dimensional-arrays\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>For an n-dimensional array with dimensions d₁×d₂×…×dₙ (row-major):</p>\n<blockquote><p>address = base + ((i₁·d₂·d₃…dₙ) + (i₂·d₃…dₙ) + … + iₙ) × size</p></blockquote>\n<p>More simply: treat it as nested arrays and apply the 2-D formula recursively.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sparse-matrices\">Sparse Matrices<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#sparse-matrices\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A matrix is <mark class=\"kw\">sparse</mark> when most of its elements are <strong>zero</strong>. Storing every zero wastes huge amounts of memory, so we store <strong>only non-zero elements</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">A 6×6 matrix with 8 non-zero entries:\n  0 0 3 0 0 0\n  0 5 0 0 0 0\n  0 0 0 0 0 9\n  1 0 0 0 0 0\n  0 0 0 2 0 6\n  0 0 0 0 0 0</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"triplet-coordinate-representation-array-of-row-col-value\">Triplet (coordinate) representation — array of (row, col, value)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#triplet-coordinate-representation-array-of-row-col-value\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Row</th><th>Col</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>5</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>3</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>6</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">struct Triple { int row, col, val; };\n// sparse = {rows=6, cols=6, nz=8, data[8]}</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"linked-list-representation\">Linked-list representation<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#linked-list-representation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Each non-zero element is a node with <code>(row, col, value)</code> and links; rows/columns can be threaded into row-headers and column-headers so insertion and deletion stay efficient.</p>\n<p><strong>Space saved</strong>: n×m elements vs (3 × nz + headers). When nz ≪ n×m, huge savings.</p>\n<p><strong>Applications</strong>: adjacency matrices of graphs, FEM/mesh problems, page-rank style web matrices, scientific computing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"searching\">Searching<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#searching\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"linear-search\">Linear search<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#linear-search\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Scan the array <strong>from the first element</strong> until the key is found or the array ends.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">for i = 0 to n-1:\n    if a[i] == key: return i       // found\nreturn -1                           // not found</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int linearSearch(int a[], int n, int key) {\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)\n        if (a[i] == key) return i;\n    return -1;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Comparisons</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Best</td><td>O(1) — key at a[0]</td></tr><tr><td>Worst</td><td>O(n) — key missing or at the end</td></tr><tr><td>Average</td><td>O(n) — (n+1)/2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Works on <strong>unsorted</strong> array; simple; no preprocessing needed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"binary-search\">Binary search<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#binary-search\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The array must be <strong>sorted</strong>. Compare the key with the <strong>middle</strong>; discard one half each step.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">low=0, high=n-1\nwhile low &lt;= high:\n    mid = (low + high) / 2\n    if a[mid] == key: return mid\n    else if a[mid] &lt; key: low = mid + 1\n    else: high = mid - 1\nreturn -1</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int binarySearch(int a[], int n, int key) {\n    int low = 0, high = n - 1;\n    while (low &lt;= high) {\n        int mid = (low + high) / 2;\n        if (a[mid] == key) return mid;\n        else if (a[mid] &lt; key) low = mid + 1;\n        else high = mid - 1;\n    }\n    return -1;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Comparisons</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Best</td><td>O(1) — key at mid initially</td></tr><tr><td>Worst / Average</td><td>O(log n)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Linear search</th><th>Binary search</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Data must be sorted</td><td>no</td><td><strong>yes</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td>Extra cost</td><td>none</td><td>must sort first</td></tr><tr><td>Works on linked list</td><td>yes</td><td>impractical</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"sorting-selection-insertion-bubble\">Sorting — Selection, Insertion, Bubble<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#sorting-selection-insertion-bubble\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"selection-sort\">Selection sort<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#selection-sort\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Repeatedly find the <strong>minimum</strong> of the unsorted part and swap it to its place.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">for i = 0 to n-2:\n    minIdx = i\n    for j = i+1 to n-1:\n        if a[j] &lt; a[minIdx]: minIdx = j\n    swap a[i], a[minIdx]</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">void selectionSort(int a[], int n) {\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n - 1; i++) {\n        int minIdx = i;\n        for (int j = i + 1; j &lt; n; j++)\n            if (a[j] &lt; a[minIdx]) minIdx = j;\n        std::swap(a[i], a[minIdx]);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Example</strong> <code>64 25 12 22 11</code> → <code>11 25 12 22 64</code> → <code>11 12 22 25 64</code>. Always O(n²) comparisons; <strong>minimum number of swaps</strong> (at most n−1). Not stable by default.</p>\n<h3 id=\"insertion-sort\">Insertion sort<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#insertion-sort\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Build the sorted part <strong>one element at a time</strong>: pick an element and insert it into its correct place among the already-sorted elements, shifting the rest.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">for i = 1 to n-1:\n    key = a[i];  j = i-1\n    while j &gt;= 0 and a[j] &gt; key:\n        a[j+1] = a[j];  j--\n    a[j+1] = key</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">void insertionSort(int a[], int n) {\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt; n; i++) {\n        int key = a[i], j = i - 1;\n        while (j &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; a[j] &gt; key) { a[j + 1] = a[j]; j--; }\n        a[j + 1] = key;\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Example</strong> <code>5 2 4 6 1 3</code> → after pass 1: <code>2 5 4 6 1 3</code> → … → <code>1 2 3 4 5 6</code>. Best case <strong>O(n)</strong> (already sorted), worst/average <strong>O(n²)</strong>. <strong>Stable</strong>, great for nearly-sorted data, excellent for small arrays.</p>\n<h3 id=\"bubble-sort\">Bubble sort<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#bubble-sort\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Repeatedly swap adjacent <strong>out-of-order</strong> pairs; after each pass the largest element \"bubbles\" to the end.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">for i = 0 to n-2:\n    for j = 0 to n-2-i:\n        if a[j] &gt; a[j+1]: swap a[j], a[j+1]</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">void bubbleSort(int a[], int n) {\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n - 1; i++)\n        for (int j = 0; j &lt; n - 1 - i; j++)\n            if (a[j] &gt; a[j + 1]) std::swap(a[j], a[j + 1]);\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p>With an early-exit flag it becomes O(n) on sorted input; worst/average <strong>O(n²)</strong>. <strong>Stable</strong>, extremely simple, but rarely used in practice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"comparison-chart\">Comparison chart<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#comparison-chart\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sort</th><th>Best</th><th>Average</th><th>Worst</th><th>Space</th><th>Stable?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Selection</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>no (typical)</td></tr><tr><td>Insertion</td><td><strong>O(n)</strong></td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>Bubble</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>Merge (Unit 2)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n)</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>Quick (Unit 2)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(log n)</td><td>no</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>\"Which sort for nearly-sorted data?\" → <strong>Insertion sort</strong> (O(n) best case, stable). \"Which sort if memory is scarce?\" → <strong>Selection sort</strong> (O(1) extra space, O(n) swaps). Never quote selection sort as stable.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"quick-revision\">Quick Revision<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-revision\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Data structure</th><th>Key idea</th><th>Typical cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Array</td><td>contiguous, indexed</td><td>access O(1), insert/delete O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Sparse matrix</td><td>store only non-zeros</td><td>memory O(nz)</td></tr><tr><td>Linear search</td><td>scan all</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Binary search</td><td>halve sorted range</td><td>O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td>Selection sort</td><td>pick min each pass</td><td>O(n²)</td></tr><tr><td>Insertion sort</td><td>insert into sorted part</td><td>O(n)–O(n²)</td></tr><tr><td>Bubble sort</td><td>swap adjacent pairs</td><td>O(n)–O(n²)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice Questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#practice-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Define data structure. Compare built-in, derived and user-defined data types.</li>\n<li>Explain best, worst and average case analysis with an example for linear search.</li>\n<li>What is the difference between O, Ω and Θ? Arrange the common growth functions in increasing order.</li>\n<li>Write a recursive C++ function to compute a<sup>n</sup>. Show the call stack for <code>power(2,4)</code>.</li>\n<li>Find the address of <code>a[4][7]</code> in a 5×10 row-major integer array with base address 1000 (4 bytes per int).</li>\n<li>Represent the given sparse matrix as triplets and as a linked list.</li>\n<li>Run binary search on <code>2 5 8 12 16 23 38 56 72 91</code> to find 23; list each mid.</li>\n<li>Trace selection, insertion and bubble sort on <code>29 10 14 37 13</code>; count the comparisons.</li>\n<li>When is insertion sort better than merge sort? Justify with complexity.</li>\n<li>Why can't binary search run on an unsorted array or a linked list efficiently?</li>\n</ol>"}, {"id": "ds-u2", "title": "DS Unit 2 — Stacks, Expressions, Merge/Quick Sort & Queues", "subject": "ds", "unit": 2, "description": "Stacks with array/linked-list implementation and operations; infix, prefix and postfix conversions and evaluation; merge sort and quick sort with worked traces; linear, circular and priority queues with implementations and applications.", "tags": "stack, infix, prefix, postfix, merge sort, quick sort, queue, circular queue, priority queue", "date": "2026-02-15", "featured": 1, "words": 1740, "reading_min": 9, "views": 341, "body_html": "<h2 id=\"stacks\">Stacks<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#stacks\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">stack</mark> is a linear structure that allows insertions and deletions <strong>only at one end (the top)</strong> — <strong>Last-In-First-Out (LIFO)</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"operations\">Operations<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#operations\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Action</th><th>Array implementation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>PUSH(x)</strong></td><td>insert x on top</td><td><code>if top == MAX-1 → overflow; a[++top] = x;</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>POP()</strong></td><td>remove top element</td><td><code>if top == -1 → underflow; return a[top--];</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>PEEK/PEEKTOP()</strong></td><td>read top, no removal</td><td><code>return a[top];</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>isEmpty()</strong></td><td>check empty</td><td><code>top == -1</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>isFull()</strong></td><td>check full</td><td><code>top == MAX-1</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>top = -1</code> is the empty-stack sentinel; PUSH increments top, POP decrements it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"array-implementation\">Array implementation<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#array-implementation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Stack {\n    int *a, top, cap;\npublic:\n    Stack(int cap_) : cap(cap_), top(-1) { a = new int[cap]; }\n    ~Stack() { delete[] a; }\n    void push(int x) {\n        if (top == cap - 1) { cout &lt;&lt; \"Overflow!\\n\"; return; }\n        a[++top] = x;\n    }\n    int pop() {\n        if (top &lt; 0) { cout &lt;&lt; \"Underflow!\\n\"; return -1; }\n        return a[top--];\n    }\n    bool isEmpty() const { return top &lt; 0; }\n};</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"linked-list-implementation\">Linked-List implementation<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#linked-list-implementation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Memory is allocated per node — no overflow until heap exhaustion. <code>top</code> is the head pointer.</p>\n<h3 id=\"applications\">Applications<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#applications\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Function calls and <strong>recursion</strong> (activation records).</li>\n<li><strong>Expression evaluation</strong> (postfix) and conversions.</li>\n<li>Undo/redo in editors, back/forward in browsers, backtracking (maze, N-queens).</li>\n<li>Balanced parentheses / delimiter matching.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"polish-notation-infix-prefix-postfix\">Polish Notation — Infix, Prefix, Postfix<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#polish-notation-infix-prefix-postfix\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Operator position</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Infix</strong></td><td>between operands</td><td><code>A + B * C</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Prefix</strong> (Polish)</td><td>before operands</td><td><code>+ A * B C</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Postfix</strong> (Reverse Polish)</td><td>after operands</td><td><code>A B C * +</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Humans read infix; <strong>computers prefer postfix</strong> (no precedence, no parentheses, single left-to-right scan).</li>\n<li>Same expression: <strong>infix</strong> <code>(A + B) * C</code> = <strong>prefix</strong> <code>* + A B C</code> = <strong>postfix</strong> <code>A B + C *</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"rules-for-infix-postfix\">Rules for infix → postfix<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#rules-for-infix-postfix\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Scan left to right; operands go straight to output.</li>\n<li>On an operator: pop output any operators on the stack with <strong>higher or equal</strong> precedence; then push it.</li>\n<li>On <code>(</code>: push. On <code>)</code>: pop until <code>(</code> (discard the <code>(</code>).</li>\n<li>End: pop everything remaining.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Precedence (high→low): <code>^</code> (exponent) > <code>* /</code> > <code>+ -</code>; associativity matters for <code>^</code> (right).</p>\n<h3 id=\"worked-example-a-b-c-d-a-b-c-d\">Worked example: <code>A + B * C − D → A B C * + D −</code><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#worked-example-a-b-c-d-a-b-c-d\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Action</th><th>Stack</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>operand → output</td><td></td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>+</td><td>push</td><td>+</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>operand</td><td>+</td><td>A B</td></tr><tr><td>*</td><td>push (higher than +)</td><td>+ *</td><td>A B</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>operand</td><td>+ *</td><td>A B C</td></tr><tr><td>−</td><td>pop <code>*</code>, pop <code>+</code>, push <code>−</code></td><td>−</td><td>A B C * +</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>operand</td><td>−</td><td>A B C * + D</td></tr><tr><td>end</td><td>pop all</td><td></td><td>A B C * + D −</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"rules-for-infix-prefix\">Rules for infix → prefix<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#rules-for-infix-prefix\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Reverse the infix string (swap <code>(</code> ↔ <code>)</code>), convert to <strong>postfix</strong>, then reverse the result.</p>\n<blockquote><p>A + B * C &rarr; reverse <code>C * B + A</code> &rarr; postfix <code>C B * A +</code> &rarr; reverse <code>+ A * B C</code> ✔</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"postfix-evaluation\">Postfix evaluation<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#postfix-evaluation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Scan left to right. Operand → push.</li>\n<li>Operator → pop two operands (b = pop, a = pop), compute <code>a op b</code>, push result.</li>\n<li>End — top of stack is the answer.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int evalPostfix(const string&amp; expr) {\n    Stack s(100);                      // assume Stack holds ints\n    for (char c : expr) {\n        if (isdigit(c)) s.push(c - '0');\n        else {\n            int b = s.pop(), a = s.pop();\n            if (c == '+') s.push(a + b);\n            if (c == '-') s.push(a - b);\n            if (c == '*') s.push(a * b);\n            if (c == '/') s.push(a / b);\n        }\n    }\n    return s.pop();\n}\n// \"23 4 +\"  →  27 ;  \"6 2 / 3 +\"  →  6</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Prefix can be evaluated by scanning <strong>right to left</strong> with the same algorithm.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"merge-sort\">Merge Sort<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#merge-sort\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Merge sort</mark> is a <strong>divide and conquer</strong>, <strong>stable</strong> sorting algorithm: split the array in half repeatedly, sort the halves, then <strong>merge</strong> them.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">MergeSort(a, low, high):\n    if low &lt; high:\n        mid = (low + high) / 2\n        MergeSort(a, low, mid)\n        MergeSort(a, mid+1, high)\n        Merge(a, low, mid, high)     // merge two sorted halves</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">void merge(int a[], int l, int m, int r) {\n    int n1 = m - l + 1, n2 = r - m;\n    int *L = new int[n1], *R = new int[n2];\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n1; i++) L[i] = a[l + i];\n    for (int j = 0; j &lt; n2; j++) R[j] = a[m + 1 + j];\n    int i = 0, j = 0, k = l;\n    while (i &lt; n1 &amp;&amp; j &lt; n2) a[k++] = (L[i] &lt;= R[j]) ? L[i++] : R[j++];\n    while (i &lt; n1) a[k++] = L[i++];\n    while (j &lt; n2) a[k++] = R[j++];\n    delete[] L; delete[] R;\n}\n\nvoid mergeSort(int a[], int l, int r) {\n    if (l &lt; r) {\n        int m = (l + r) / 2;\n        mergeSort(a, l, m);\n        mergeSort(a, m + 1, r);\n        merge(a, l, m, r);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Worked trace</strong> <code>38 27 43 3</code></p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">[38 27 43 3]\n├─ [38 27] ─ [38] [27] ─ merge → [27 38]\n└─ [43 3]  ─ [43] [3]  ─ merge → [3 43]\n        merge [27 38] + [3 43] → [3 27 38 43]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Complexity</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Best / Average / Worst</td><td><strong>O(n log n)</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Space</td><td>O(n) auxiliary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"quick-sort\">Quick Sort<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-sort\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Quick sort</mark> is a <strong>divide and conquer</strong>, <strong>in-place</strong> (O(log n) stack space) algorithm: pick a <strong>pivot</strong>, partition the array so smaller elements go left and larger go right, then recurse on both parts.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">QuickSort(a, low, high):\n    if low &lt; high:\n        pi = Partition(a, low, high)   // pivot lands in place\n        QuickSort(a, low, pi-1)\n        QuickSort(a, pi+1, high)\n\nPartition (Lomuto, pivot = a[high]):\n    i = low - 1\n    for j = low to high-1:\n        if a[j] &lt; pivot: i++; swap a[i], a[j]\n    swap a[i+1], a[high]\n    return i+1</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int partition(int a[], int lo, int hi) {\n    int pivot = a[hi], i = lo - 1;\n    for (int j = lo; j &lt; hi; j++) {\n        if (a[j] &lt; pivot) { i++; std::swap(a[i], a[j]); }\n    }\n    std::swap(a[i + 1], a[hi]);\n    return i + 1;\n}\n\nvoid quickSort(int a[], int lo, int hi) {\n    if (lo &lt; hi) {\n        int pi = partition(a, lo, hi);\n        quickSort(a, lo, pi - 1);\n        quickSort(a, pi + 1, hi);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Worked trace</strong> <code>10 80 30 90 40 50 70</code> (pivot = last element)</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pass</th><th>Partition result</th><th>Recursive calls</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><code>10 30 40 50 70 90 80</code> (pivot 70 at index 4)</td><td>left <code>10 30 40 50</code>, right <code>90 80</code></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>left → <code>10 30 40 50</code> (pivot 50); right → <code>80 90</code></td><td>…</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>sorted: <code>10 30 40 50 70 80 90</code></td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Complexity</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Best / Average</td><td><strong>O(n log n)</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Worst</td><td><strong>O(n²)</strong> (already sorted with bad pivot choice — pick middle/random pivot)</td></tr><tr><td>Space</td><td>O(log n) (stack)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Classic interview/exam trap: quick sort worst case is O(n²) when the pivot is always the smallest/largest element (e.g., already-sorted input with a naive pivot). Merge sort is always O(n log n) but needs O(n) extra space. Choose accordingly.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"queues\">Queues<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#queues\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">queue</mark> is a linear structure with insertions at the <strong>rear</strong> and deletions at the <strong>front</strong> — <strong>First-In-First-Out (FIFO)</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"operations-1\">Operations<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#operations-1\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Array implementation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>ENQUEUE(x)</strong></td><td><code>if rear == MAX-1 → overflow; a[++rear] = x;</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>DEQUEUE()</strong></td><td><code>if front &gt; rear → underflow; return a[front++];</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>isEmpty()</strong></td><td><code>front &gt; rear</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"sequential-linear-queue-problem\">Sequential linear queue problem<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#sequential-linear-queue-problem\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>With <code>a[++rear]</code> insertion and <code>front++</code> removal, deleted cells at the front are <strong>never reused</strong> — the queue \"drifts\" to the right and reports overflow while the front is empty. → <strong>Circular queue</strong> fixes this.</p>\n<h2 id=\"circular-queue\">Circular Queue<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#circular-queue\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The array wraps around: <code>rear = (rear + 1) % MAX</code>, <code>front = (front + 1) % MAX</code>. One slot is left empty to distinguish empty from full.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class CircularQueue {\n    int *a, front, rear, cap, count;\npublic:\n    bool enqueue(int x) {\n        if (count == cap) return false;      // full\n        a[rear] = x;\n        rear = (rear + 1) % cap;             // wrap\n        count++;\n        return true;\n    }\n    int dequeue() {\n        if (count == 0) return -1;           // empty\n        int x = a[front];\n        front = (front + 1) % cap;           // wrap\n        count--;\n        return x;\n    }\n};</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Linear queue</th><th>Circular queue</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Space reuse</td><td>no — front cells wasted</td><td>yes — wraps around</td></tr><tr><td>full condition</td><td><code>rear == MAX-1</code></td><td><code>count == cap</code> (or front–rear pattern)</td></tr><tr><td>Complexity</td><td>all O(1)</td><td>all O(1)</td></tr><tr><td>Use</td><td>teaching, simple</td><td>real buffers, scheduling</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"priority-queue\">Priority Queue<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#priority-queue\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>In a <mark class=\"kw\">priority queue</mark>, <strong>dequeue always removes the highest (or lowest) priority</strong> element, regardless of arrival order. Implementations:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Implementation</th><th>Enqueue</th><th>Dequeue</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unordered array/list</td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(n) scan for max</td><td>simple</td></tr><tr><td>Sorted array</td><td>O(n) insertion</td><td>O(1)</td><td>one end is fixed (unless deque)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Binary heap</strong></td><td><strong>O(log n)</strong></td><td><strong>O(log n)</strong></td><td>the standard choice</td></tr><tr><td>Linked list</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>keep sorted</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">// Min-heap based priority queue (standard library)\n#include &lt;queue&gt;\nstd::priority_queue&lt;int, std::vector&lt;int&gt;, std::greater&lt;int&gt;&gt; pq; // min at top\npq.push(30); pq.push(10); pq.push(20);\n// top() = 10 ; pop() removes 10, then 20, then 30</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Applications of priority queues</strong>: Dijkstra's shortest path, scheduling (OS), Huffman coding, A* search, patient triage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"applications-of-queues\">Applications of queues<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#applications-of-queues\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>CPU / process <strong>scheduling</strong> (FCFS), print spooling.</li>\n<li><strong>Breadth-First Search</strong> in graphs.</li>\n<li>Buffering: keyboard, network packets, producer-consumer.</li>\n<li>Waiting lines/queuing systems, breadth-first traversal of trees.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"quick-revision\">Quick Revision<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-revision\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Rule</th><th>Implementations</th><th>Typical cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stack</td><td>LIFO, push/pop at top</td><td>array, linked list</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td>Postfix eval</td><td>single scan + stack</td><td></td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Merge sort</td><td>divide, sort, merge</td><td></td><td>O(n log n) time, O(n) space</td></tr><tr><td>Quick sort</td><td>pivot + partition</td><td></td><td>O(n log n) avg</td></tr><tr><td>Linear queue</td><td>FIFO</td><td>array</td><td>O(1) but wasted space</td></tr><tr><td>Circular queue</td><td>wrap around</td><td>array % cap</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td>Priority queue</td><td>highest priority first</td><td>heap</td><td>O(log n)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice Questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#practice-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Implement a stack using an array; write PUSH, POP, PEEK with overflow/underflow checks.</li>\n<li>Convert <code>A * (B + C) / D</code> to postfix, showing the stack at every step.</li>\n<li>Evaluate <code>5 3 2 * + 8 4 / -</code> and state the result.</li>\n<li>Convert <code>(A − B) * (C + D)</code> to prefix.</li>\n<li>Trace merge sort on <code>12 11 13 5 6 7</code>; state its time and space complexity.</li>\n<li>Trace quick sort on <code>9 4 7 3 8 2 5</code>; explain the worst case.</li>\n<li>Differentiate a linear queue from a circular queue with a diagram.</li>\n<li>Implement ENQUEUE/DEQUEUE for a circular queue with front/rear wraparound.</li>\n<li>What is a priority queue? Compare array, sorted list and heap implementations.</li>\n<li>Give five real applications of stacks and five of queues.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"id": "it-u1", "title": "IT Unit 1 — Python Basics, Tokens, Data Types & Errors", "subject": "it", "unit": 1, "description": "Introduction to Python, its features, interactive vs script execution modes, character set, indentation, tokens (keywords, identifiers, literals, operators, punctuators), variables, comments, number data types and the three kinds of errors — with example programs.", "tags": "python, interactive mode, script mode, tokens, identifiers, comments, data types, errors", "date": "2026-02-08", "featured": 1, "words": 1122, "reading_min": 6, "views": 322, "body_html": "<h2 id=\"introduction-to-python\">Introduction to Python<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#introduction-to-python\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Python</mark> is a high-level, general-purpose, <strong>interpreted</strong> programming language created by <strong>Guido van Rossum</strong> (1991). It emphasizes code <strong>readability</strong> and fast <strong>development</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"features-of-python\">Features of Python<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#features-of-python\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Interpreted</strong> — executes line by line; no separate compilation step.</li>\n<li><strong>Object oriented</strong> — supports classes, objects, inheritance, polymorphism.</li>\n<li><strong>Simple and readable</strong> — clean syntax, uses <strong>indentation</strong> instead of braces.</li>\n<li><strong>Dynamically typed</strong> — the type of a variable is decided at runtime; you don't declare types.</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-platform</strong> — Windows, Linux, macOS.</li>\n<li><strong>Huge standard library</strong> — files, math, networking, databases, web.</li>\n<li><strong>Free and open source</strong>, huge community.</li>\n<li><strong>Extensible &amp; embeddable</strong> — can call C/C++ libraries.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"execution-modes\">Execution modes<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#execution-modes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>How</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Interactive</strong></td><td>type one statement, see the result immediately (REPL — Read-Evaluate-Print-Loop)</td><td><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; 2 + 3</code> gives <code>5</code> at once</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Script</strong></td><td>write a <code>.py</code> file, run the whole program</td><td><code>python program.py</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Shell</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># interactive\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print(\"Hello\")\nHello\n\n# script mode  (hello.py)\nprint(\"Hello, World!\")         # run with:  python hello.py</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Use <strong>interactive mode</strong> for quick experiments and testing one-liners; use <strong>script mode</strong> for real programs.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"python-character-set\">Python character set<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#python-character-set\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The valid set of characters includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Letters: <strong>A–Z, a–z</strong> (and Unicode alphabets in Python 3)</li>\n<li>Digits: <strong>0–9</strong></li>\n<li>Whitespace: space, tab, newline</li>\n<li>Operators and special symbols: <code>+ - * / % ** // = &lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;= == != ( ) [ ] { } , . : ; @ # ' \" \\n _</code></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"indentation\">Indentation<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#indentation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Indentation</mark> (leading spaces/tabs) is <strong>syntactically meaningful</strong>: it groups statements into blocks. Consistent indentation is mandatory.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">marks = 85\nif marks &gt;= 75:\n    print(\"Distinction\")   # inside the if-block (indented)\nprint(\"Done\")              # outside the block</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Mixing tabs and spaces, or inconsistent indent levels, raises <code>IndentationError</code>. Use <strong>4 spaces</strong> consistently.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"python-tokens\">Python Tokens<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#python-tokens\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">token</mark> is the smallest meaningful unit of a program. The scanner breaks source text into tokens.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Token type</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Keyword</strong></td><td><code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, <code>while</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>def</code>, <code>class</code>, <code>import</code>, <code>return</code>, <code>True</code>, <code>False</code>, <code>None</code>, <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>not</code>, <code>in</code>, <code>is</code>, <code>lambda</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Identifier</strong></td><td>names you define: <code>age</code>, <code>total_marks</code>, <code>_count</code>, <code>Student</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Literal</strong></td><td><code>23</code>, <code>3.14</code>, <code>\"hi\"</code>, <code>'c'</code>, <code>True</code>, <code>None</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Operator</strong></td><td><code>+ - * / % // ** = == &lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;= and or not</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Punctuator</strong></td><td><code>( ) [ ] { } , : ; .</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"variables\">Variables<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#variables\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">variable</mark> is a <strong>name that refers to a value</strong> stored in memory. No declaration or type is needed — assignment creates and (re)types it.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">age = 19                # integer variable\nname = \"Aman\"           # string variable\nmarks = 88.5            # float variable\nage = \"nineteen\"        # legal! variable can change type (dynamic typing)</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Identifier rules:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>May contain letters, digits and <code>_</code>; must <strong>not start with a digit</strong>.</li>\n<li>Cannot be a <strong>keyword</strong> (<code>if</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>class</code>, …).</li>\n<li>Case sensitive: <code>Age</code> and <code>age</code> differ.</li>\n<li>Use meaningful names: <code>student_name</code>, not <code>sn</code>.</li>\n<li>Convention: <code>snake_case</code> for variables/functions, <code>PascalCase</code> for classes.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\"># valid identifiers\n_name, total_marks2, page_Count, MinValue\n# invalid identifiers\n2name, my-name, class, if, name!</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"comments\">Comments<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#comments\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Comments</mark> are ignored by the interpreter — they explain code to humans.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\"># single line comment\n\n\"\"\"\nMulti-line comment /\ndocstring — conventionally placed\nat the top of a file or function.\n\"\"\"</code></pre></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><code>#</code> starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.</li>\n<li>Triple-quoted strings (<code>\"\"\"…\"\"\"</code> or <code>'''…'''</code>) act as multi-line comments/docstrings.</li>\n<li>Comments help debugging: you can temporarily disable a line by commenting it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"number-data-types\">Number Data Types<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#number-data-types\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Examples</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>int</code></td><td>whole numbers (unlimited precision)</td><td><code>42</code>, <code>-7</code>, <code>0</code>, <code>10**100</code></td><td>no size limit in Python 3</td></tr><tr><td><code>float</code></td><td>decimals / real numbers</td><td><code>3.14</code>, <code>-0.5</code>, <code>2.0</code>, <code>1e6</code></td><td>stored as double precision</td></tr><tr><td><code>complex</code></td><td>a + bj</td><td><code>3 + 4j</code>, <code>complex(2, 5)</code></td><td><code>j</code>/<code>J</code> is the imaginary unit</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">a = 10            # int\nb = 3.142         # float\nc = 2 + 5j        # complex\n\nprint(type(a))    # &lt;class 'int'&gt;\nprint(type(b))    # &lt;class 'float'&gt;\nprint(type(c))    # &lt;class 'complex'&gt;\nprint(c.real, c.imag)   # 2.0 5.0</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Note</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><code>type(x)</code> returns the data type of <code>x</code> — one of the most used built-in functions during learning.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"arithmetic-that-produces-numbers\">Arithmetic that produces numbers<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#arithmetic-that-produces-numbers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">print(7 // 2)    # 3      floor division\nprint(7 % 2)     # 1      remainder\nprint(2 ** 5)    # 32     exponent\nprint(10 / 4)    # 2.5    true division (always float)\nprint(5 + 2.0)   # 7.0    int + float -&gt; float</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"errors-in-python\">Errors in Python<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#errors-in-python\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>What it is</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Syntax error</strong></td><td>violates the <strong>grammar</strong> of Python; the program <strong>cannot run</strong></td><td><code>print(\"hi\"</code> (missing <code>)</code>), <code>if x &gt; 5 print(x)</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Logical error</strong></td><td>the program <strong>runs</strong> but gives a <strong>wrong result</strong>; no message</td><td>computing average as <code>sum / n - 1</code> instead of <code>sum / n</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Runtime error</strong> (exception)</td><td>the program <strong>crashes while running</strong></td><td><code>10 / 0</code> → <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>, <code>int(\"abc\")</code> → <code>ValueError</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\"># 1. Syntax error — Python refuses to run\n# print(\"Hello\"\n\n# 2. Logical error — runs, wrong answer\nn = 3\nresult = 100 / n - 4        # meant 100 / (n - 4)? gives 29.33 instead of error/intent\nprint(result)\n\n# 3. Runtime error\n# print(10 / 0)             # ZeroDivisionError: division by zero</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>To fix a <strong>logical error</strong>, trace values with <code>print()</code> or use <code>type()</code> to check what variables actually contain. Read the full <strong>traceback</strong> for runtime errors — the last line names the exception and the exact line number.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"quick-revision\">Quick Revision<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-revision\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>One-liner</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Python</td><td>interpreted, OO, high-level, indentation-based</td></tr><tr><td>Interactive mode</td><td>REPL — immediate results</td></tr><tr><td>Script mode</td><td>run a <code>.py</code> file</td></tr><tr><td>Tokens</td><td>keywords, identifiers, literals, operators, punctuators</td></tr><tr><td>Variables</td><td>dynamically typed names bound to values</td></tr><tr><td><code>int</code> / <code>float</code> / <code>complex</code></td><td>whole / decimal / a+bj numbers</td></tr><tr><td>Comments</td><td><code>#</code> and <code>\"\"\" docstrings \"\"\"</code></td></tr><tr><td>Error types</td><td>syntax (won't run), logical (wrong result), runtime (crash)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice Questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#practice-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write any five features of Python.</li>\n<li>Distinguish interactive mode from script mode with an example program each.</li>\n<li>List the five categories of tokens. Give three examples of each.</li>\n<li>Which of these are valid identifiers: <code>2score</code>, <code>_name</code>, <code>student name</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>Marks2</code>, <code>class</code>? Justify.</li>\n<li>What is the role of indentation in Python? What error occurs if indentation is wrong?</li>\n<li>Explain <code>int</code>, <code>float</code> and <code>complex</code> with examples and find their <code>type()</code>.</li>\n<li>Predict the output of <code>7 // 2</code>, <code>7 % 2</code>, <code>7 / 2</code>, <code>2 ** 3</code>, <code>\"a\" * 3</code>.</li>\n<li>Differentiate syntax, logical and runtime errors with one program for each.</li>\n<li>Write a script that reads a student's name and two marks and prints the average.</li>\n<li>Why is Python called dynamically typed? Contrast with a statically typed language like C++.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"id": "it-u2", "title": "IT Unit 2 — Expressions, Operators, Control Flow & Strings", "subject": "it", "unit": 2, "description": "Statements, expressions, type conversion, input/output, operator precedence, arithmetic/relational/logical/assignment/augmented/identity operators, evaluating expressions, if-elif-else and loops, plus strings — operations, traversal and all the built-in string methods with example programs.", "tags": "python, operators, precedence, type conversion, input output, if elif else, loops, strings, string methods", "date": "2026-02-13", "featured": 1, "words": 1406, "reading_min": 7, "views": 305, "body_html": "<h2 id=\"statements-and-expressions\">Statements and Expressions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#statements-and-expressions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A <mark class=\"kw\">statement</mark> is one instruction the interpreter executes: <code>name = \"Aman\"</code>, <code>print(x)</code>, <code>if x &gt; 0:</code>.</li>\n<li>An <mark class=\"kw\">expression</mark> is a combination of values, variables and operators that <strong>produces a value</strong>: <code>2 + 3 * 4</code>, <code>x ** 2</code>, <code>len(s)</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">x = 10                       # statement\ny = x * 2 + 5                # statement; RHS is an expression → 25\nprint(y)                     # statement (calls a function)</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"input-and-output\">Input and Output<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#input-and-output\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>input(prompt)</code></td><td>always returns a <strong>string</strong> from the keyboard</td></tr><tr><td><code>print(*values, sep=' ', end='\\n')</code></td><td>writes values to the console</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">name = input(\"Enter your name: \")     # returns a string\nage = int(input(\"Enter age: \"))       # convert string -&gt; int\nprint(\"Hello\", name, \"Age\", age, sep=\" | \")</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><code>input()</code> returns a <strong>string</strong>. Comparing it with a number, or adding it mathematically, fails until you convert with <code>int()</code>, <code>float()</code> or <code>str()</code>.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"type-conversion\">Type Conversion<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#type-conversion\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Implicit</strong></td><td>Python converts automatically (only widening)</td><td><code>5 + 2.0</code> → <code>7.0</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Explicit</strong> (casting)</td><td>you convert deliberately</td><td><code>int(\"42\")</code>, <code>float(\"3.14\")</code>, <code>str(100)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">s = \"3.75\"\nmarks = float(s)                    # 3.75\ntotal = int(\"10\") + 5               # 15\ntext = str(100) + \" students\"       # \"100 students\"\nx = int(\"abc\")                      # ValueError: invalid literal</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Python data types have an implicit precedence when mixed: <code>bool &lt; int &lt; float &lt; complex</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"operators-and-precedence\">Operators and Precedence<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#operators-and-precedence\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"arithmetic-operators\">Arithmetic operators<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#arithmetic-operators\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>+ - *</code></td><td>add, subtract, multiply</td><td><code>7 - 3 * 2</code></td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><code>/</code></td><td>true division</td><td><code>7 / 2</code></td><td>3.5</td></tr><tr><td><code>//</code></td><td>floor division</td><td><code>7 // 2</code>, <code>-7 // 2</code></td><td>3, −4</td></tr><tr><td><code>%</code></td><td>modulus (remainder)</td><td><code>7 % 2</code></td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><code>**</code></td><td>exponent</td><td><code>2 ** 10</code></td><td>1024</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"relational-comparison-operators\">Relational (comparison) operators<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#relational-comparison-operators\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>==</code>, <code>!=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>&lt;=</code> — result is a <strong>bool</strong> (<code>True</code>/<code>False</code>). Strings compare <strong>lexicographically</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">print(5 &gt; 3)      # True\nprint(5 == 3)     # False\nprint(\"abc\" &lt; \"abd\")   # True (a==a, b==b, c&lt;d)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"logical-operators\">Logical operators<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#logical-operators\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Truth table</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>and</code></td><td>True if <strong>both</strong> True</td><td><code>T and T = T</code>, else F</td></tr><tr><td><code>or</code></td><td>True if <strong>either</strong> True</td><td><code>F or F = F</code>, else T</td></tr><tr><td><code>not</code></td><td>negates</td><td><code>not True = False</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">age = 20\nhas_id = True\nprint(age &gt;= 18 and has_id)   # True\nprint(age &gt; 60 or age &lt; 12)   # False\nprint(not has_id)             # False</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"assignment-operators\">Assignment operators<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#assignment-operators\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><code>=</code> assigns; <strong>augmented assignment</strong> combines an operation with assignment:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">x = 10\nx += 5     # x = x + 5 → 15\nx -= 2     # 13\nx *= 3     # 39\nx /= 2     # 19.5\nx //= 2    # 9.0\nx %= 4     # 1.0\nx **= 3    # 1.0</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"identity-operators-is-and-is-not\">Identity operators — <code>is</code> and <code>is not</code><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#identity-operators-is-and-is-not\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Compare whether two names refer to the <strong>same object in memory</strong> (not whether values are equal).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">a = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a            # b refers to the SAME list\nc = [1, 2, 3]    # a different list with equal values\nprint(a is b)    # True\nprint(a is c)    # False\nprint(a == c)    # True   (value equality)</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Use <code>is</code>/<code>is not</code> for <strong>None</strong> and identity checks; use <code>==</code>/<code>!=</code> for comparing values. For small integers Python interns values, so <code>5 is 5</code> may be <code>True</code> — don't rely on it.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"operator-precedence-high-low\">Operator Precedence (high → low)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#operator-precedence-high-low\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Operators</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><code>( )</code> parentheses, <code>**</code></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>unary <code>+x -x</code>, <code>~x</code></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><code>* / // %</code></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><code>+ -</code></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><code>== != &gt; &lt; &gt;= &lt;=</code></td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><code>not</code></td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><code>and</code></td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><code>or</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Evaluate <code>2 + 3 * 4 ** 2</code> = <code>2 + 3 * 16</code> = <code>2 + 48</code> = <strong>50</strong>. When in doubt, add parentheses — they are free and make intent clear.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"flow-of-control\">Flow of Control<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#flow-of-control\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"conditional-statements\">Conditional statements<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#conditional-statements\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">marks = int(input(\"Marks: \"))\n\nif marks &gt;= 90:\n    grade = \"A\"\nelif marks &gt;= 75:\n    grade = \"B\"\nelif marks &gt;= 60:\n    grade = \"C\"\nelse:\n    grade = \"D\"\nprint(\"Grade:\", grade)</code></pre></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><code>if</code> / <code>elif</code> / <code>else</code> — mutually exclusive branches.</li>\n<li>Nested <code>if</code> is allowed (an <code>if</code> inside an <code>if</code> block).</li>\n<li>Any truthy value (non-zero number, non-empty string/list) makes a condition <code>True</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"iterative-statements-loops\">Iterative statements (loops)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#iterative-statements-loops\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\"># for loop — definite iteration\nfor i in range(1, 6):\n    print(i, end=\" \")          # 1 2 3 4 5\n\n# range(start, stop, step)\nfor i in range(10, 0, -2):\n    print(i, end=\" \")          # 10 8 6 4 2\n\n# while loop — indefinite iteration\nn = 5\nwhile n &gt; 0:\n    print(n, end=\" \")\n    n -= 1                     # 5 4 3 2 1\n\n# break / continue\nfor i in range(1, 10):\n    if i == 3: continue        # skip 3\n    if i == 7: break           # stop at 7\n    print(i, end=\" \")          # 1 2 4 5 6</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Note</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><code>range(n)</code> → 0..n−1; <code>range(a, b)</code> → a..b−1; <code>range(a, b, s)</code> → step s. It is lazy (produces numbers on demand).</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"strings\">Strings<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#strings\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">string</mark> is an <strong>immutable sequence of characters</strong> enclosed in <code>' '</code>, <code>\" \"</code>, or <code>''' '''</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">s1 = 'hello'\ns2 = \"world\"\ns3 = '''multi\nline'''</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"string-operations\">String operations<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#string-operations\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Example</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Concatenation <code>+</code></td><td><code>\"Py\" + \"thon\"</code></td><td><code>\"Python\"</code></td></tr><tr><td>Repetition <code>*</code></td><td><code>\"Ha\" * 3</code></td><td><code>\"HaHaHa\"</code></td></tr><tr><td>Membership <code>in</code> / <code>not in</code></td><td><code>\"ell\" in \"hello\"</code></td><td><code>True</code></td></tr><tr><td>Slicing <code>[start:stop:step]</code></td><td><code>\"hello\"[1:4]</code></td><td><code>\"ell\"</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">word = \"Python\"\nprint(word[0])        # P\nprint(word[-1])       # n   (negative index: from the end)\nprint(word[0:4])      # Pyth  (start:stop — stop is exclusive)\nprint(word[::2])      # Pto   (every 2nd character)\nprint(word[::-1])     # nohtyP (reversed)</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Strings are <strong>immutable</strong> — <code>word[0] = \"J\"</code> raises <code>TypeError</code>. \"Changing\" a string means creating a new one: <code>word = \"J\" + word[1:]</code>.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"traversing-a-string-using-loops\">Traversing a string using loops<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#traversing-a-string-using-loops\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">name = \"Riya\"\nfor ch in name:              # character by character\n    print(ch, end=\"-\")       # R-i-y-a-\n\nfor i in range(len(name)):   # index based\n    print(name[i], end=\" \")  # R i y a</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"built-in-functions-and-string-methods\">Built-in functions and string methods<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#built-in-functions-and-string-methods\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">s = \"hello world\"\n\nlen(s)                # 11\ns.capitalize()        # \"Hello world\"\ns.title()             # \"Hello World\"\ns.upper()             # \"HELLO WORLD\"\ns.lower()             # \"hello world\"\ns.count(\"l\")          # 3\ns.find(\"world\")       # 6  (first index or -1)\ns.index(\"world\")      # 6  (raises ValueError if absent)\ns.endswith(\"world\")   # True\ns.startswith(\"hello\") # True\n\"abc123\".isalnum()    # True  (letters + digits)\n\"abc\".isalpha()       # True  (only letters)\n\"123\".isdigit()       # True  (only digits)\n\"abc\".islower()       # True\n\"ABC\".isupper()       # True\n\"   \".isspace()       # True\n\"  hi  \".lstrip()     # \"hi  \"  (strip left)\n\"  hi  \".rstrip()     # \"  hi\"\n\"  hi  \".strip()      # \"hi\"\ns.replace(\"world\", \"Python\")   # \"hello Python\"\n\"-\".join([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])      # \"a-b-c\"\n\"name:age:city\".partition(\":\") # (\"name\", \":\", \"age:city\")\n\"a b c\".split()                # ['a', 'b', 'c']  (split on space)</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Count vowels in a string:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-python\">text = \"python programming\"\ncount = 0\nfor ch in text.lower():\n    if ch in \"aeiou\":\n        count += 1\nprint(count)      # 4</code></pre></figure></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><code>find()</code> returns <strong>−1</strong> when absent; <code>index()</code> raises an error. Use <code>find()</code> when the value may be missing, <code>index()</code> when you know it exists.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"quick-revision\">Quick Revision<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-revision\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>One-liner</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Expression vs statement</td><td>expression yields a value; statement is an action</td></tr><tr><td>Type conversion</td><td>implicit (automatic) vs explicit (<code>int()</code>, <code>float()</code>, <code>str()</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>// % **</code></td><td>floor divide, remainder, exponent</td></tr><tr><td><code>is</code> vs <code>==</code></td><td>identity (same object) vs value equality</td></tr><tr><td>Precedence</td><td><code>**</code> → <code>* / // %</code> → <code>+ -</code> → comparisons → <code>not and or</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>if/elif/else</code></td><td>multi-way conditional</td></tr><tr><td>Loops</td><td><code>for</code> (range/sequence) and <code>while</code> (condition)</td></tr><tr><td>String immutability</td><td>cannot modify — create a new string</td></tr><tr><td>Slicing</td><td><code>[start:stop:step]</code>, negative indices, <code>[::-1]</code> reverse</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice Questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#practice-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li>What is the difference between a statement and an expression? Give three examples of each.</li>\n<li>Trace <code>value = 2 + 3 * 4 ** 2 - 8 / 4 % 3</code> giving each step with precedence.</li>\n<li>Write a program reading <code>n</code> and printing the sum of the first <code>n</code> natural numbers.</li>\n<li>Write a marks→grade program using <code>if/elif/else</code>.</li>\n<li>Explain <code>is</code> vs <code>==</code> with two lists. When should each be used?</li>\n<li>Write output of <code>\"Py\" * 2 + \"thon\"</code>, <code>\"abc\"[::-1]</code>, <code>\"hello world\".title()</code>.</li>\n<li>Write a program to count vowels and consonants in a string using a <code>for</code> loop.</li>\n<li>Show the difference between <code>\"  x  \".strip()</code>, <code>.lstrip()</code> and <code>.rstrip()</code>.</li>\n<li>Convert <code>\"1,2,3,4\"</code> into the list <code>[1, 2, 3, 4]</code> using <code>split()</code> and <code>int()</code>.</li>\n<li>Why can <code>s[0] = 'J'</code> fail? How do you actually \"change\" a string?</li>\n</ol>"}, {"id": "math-formulas", "title": "Math — Probability & Statistics Formula Sheet", "subject": "math", "unit": 0, "description": "One-page quick-revision card — every formula from Unit I (probability laws, conditional probability, independence, Bayes' theorem, random variables, PMF, CDF, moments, expectation, variance and standard deviation) with one-line usage notes.", "tags": "formula sheet, probability, bayes, expectation, variance, revision", "date": "2026-02-17", "featured": 0, "words": 486, "reading_min": 2, "views": 274, "body_html": "<h2 id=\"quick-reference-card-probability\">Quick Reference Card — Probability<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-reference-card-probability\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"counting-basics\">Counting &amp; basics<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#counting-basics\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Complement</td><td>P(E′) = 1 − P(E)</td></tr><tr><td>Classical probability</td><td>P(E) = n(E) / n(S)</td></tr><tr><td>Certain / impossible</td><td>P(S) = 1 ; P(∅) = 0</td></tr><tr><td>Axioms</td><td>0 ≤ P(E) ≤ 1; P(S) = 1; additivity</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"additive-law-or\">Additive law (OR)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#additive-law-or\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Any two events</td><td>P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B)</td></tr><tr><td>Mutually exclusive</td><td>P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B)</td></tr><tr><td>Three events</td><td>P(A) + P(B) + P(C) − P(A∩B) − P(B∩C) − P(A∩C) + P(A∩B∩C)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"conditional-probability-independence\">Conditional probability &amp; independence<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#conditional-probability-independence\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"formula\"> P(A | B) = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"num\">P(A ∩ B)</span><span class=\"den\">P(B)</span></span> </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Multiplication rule</td><td>P(A ∩ B) = P(B)·P(A</td><td>B) = P(A)·P(B</td><td>A)</td></tr><tr><td>Independence</td><td>P(A ∩ B) = P(A)·P(B) ⇔ P(A</td><td>B) = P(A) ⇔ P(B</td><td>A) = P(B)</td></tr><tr><td>Independent \"OR\"</td><td>P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A)·P(B)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"bayes-theorem\">Bayes' theorem<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#bayes-theorem\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> P(Ak | B) = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"num\">P(Ak)·P(B | Ak)</span><span class=\"den\">Σ<sub>j</sub> P(Aj)·P(B | Aj)</span></span> </div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Divide the <strong>required branch product</strong> by the <strong>sum of branch products</strong> (the total probability of B). The Aj must partition the sample space.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"quick-reference-card-random-variables\">Quick Reference Card — Random Variables<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-reference-card-random-variables\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Formula</th><th>Example value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PMF</td><td>p(x) = P(X = x), Σ p(x) = 1</td><td>heads in 3 tosses: 1/8,3/8,3/8,1/8</td></tr><tr><td>CDF</td><td>F(x) = P(X ≤ x) = Σ<sub>t≤x</sub> p(t)</td><td>F(2) = 7/8</td></tr><tr><td>Mean / expectation</td><td>E(X) = Σ x·p(x)</td><td>1.5</td></tr><tr><td>r-th moment</td><td>μ′r = E(X<sup>r</sup>) = Σ x<sup>r</sup> p(x)</td><td>E(X²) = 3</td></tr><tr><td>Variance</td><td>Var(X) = E(X²) − [E(X)]²</td><td>0.75</td></tr><tr><td>Standard deviation</td><td>σ = √Var(X)</td><td>0.866</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"linearity-rules\">Linearity rules<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#linearity-rules\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Linear transform</td><td>E(aX + b) = aE(X) + b</td></tr><tr><td>Variance transform</td><td>Var(aX + b) = a²Var(X)</td></tr><tr><td>Sum of means</td><td>E(X + Y) = E(X) + E(Y) (always)</td></tr><tr><td>Sum of variances (independent)</td><td>Var(X + Y) = Var(X) + Var(Y)</td></tr><tr><td>Difference (independent)</td><td>Var(X − Y) = Var(X) + Var(Y)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"worked-mini-example-single-fair-die\">Worked mini-example (single fair die)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#worked-mini-example-single-fair-die\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>X = 1,2,…,6 each with p = 1/6.</p>\n<div class=\"formula\"> E(X) = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"num\">1+2+3+4+5+6</span><span class=\"den\">6</span></span> = 3.5 </div>\n<div class=\"formula\"> E(X²) = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"num\">1+4+9+16+25+36</span><span class=\"den\">6</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"num\">91</span><span class=\"den\">6</span></span> </div>\n<div class=\"formula\"> Var(X) = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"num\">91</span><span class=\"den\">6</span></span> − (3.5)² = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"num\">35</span><span class=\"den\">12</span></span> ≈ 2.917,&nbsp; σ = √(<span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"num\">35</span><span class=\"den\">12</span></span>) ≈ 1.708 </div>\n<h2 id=\"common-pitfalls\">Common pitfalls<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#common-pitfalls\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>Correction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Quoting P(A∪B) = P(A) + P(B) always</td><td>subtract P(A∩B) unless mutually exclusive</td></tr><tr><td>Treating P(A</td><td>B) = P(B</td><td>A)</td><td>Bayes' theorem reverses them</td></tr><tr><td>Using Var(X) = E(X²) − E(X)</td><td>it is <strong>E(X²) − [E(X)]²</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting Σp(x) = 1 check</td><td>verify your PMF — it finds mistakes fast</td></tr><tr><td>Confusing sample space with event</td><td>S is the <em>whole</em> set; an event is a subset</td></tr><tr><td>Independence ≠ disjointness</td><td>disjoint (mutually exclusive) events are never independent (unless zero-probability)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"last-minute-revision-list\">Last-minute revision list<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#last-minute-revision-list\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Can you write P(A∪B), P(A∩B), P(A|B), P(B|A) for the same example?</li>\n<li>Can you derive Bayes' theorem from the multiplication rule in one line?</li>\n<li>Can you compute E(X), E(X²), Var(X), σ from any PMF table?</li>\n<li>Do you know when Var(X+Y) = Var(X)+Var(Y) requires independence?</li>\n<li>Can you state the three Kolmogorov axioms?</li>\n</ol>"}, {"id": "math-u1", "title": "Math Unit I — Probability & Discrete Random Variables", "subject": "math", "unit": 1, "description": "Basic probability — sample spaces, the additive law, conditional probability, independent events and Bayes' theorem — then discrete random variables: probability distributions, PMF, distribution function, expectation, moments, variance and standard deviation — with formulas and solved examples.", "tags": "probability, additive law, conditional probability, independent events, bayes theorem, random variables, pmf, expectation, variance", "date": "2026-02-06", "featured": 1, "words": 1350, "reading_min": 7, "views": 366, "body_html": "<h2 id=\"basic-probability\">Basic Probability<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#basic-probability\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"random-experiment-sample-space-event\">Random experiment, sample space, event<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#random-experiment-sample-space-event\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example (toss a coin twice)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><mark class=\"kw\">Random experiment</mark></td><td>process with more than one possible outcome, repeatable</td><td>tossing a coin 2 times</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"kw\">Outcome</mark></td><td>one possible result</td><td><code>H T</code></td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"kw\">Sample space</mark> S</td><td>set of all outcomes</td><td>{HH, HT, TH, TT}</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"kw\">Event</mark> E</td><td>a subset of S</td><td>E = \"at least one head\" = {HH, HT, TH}</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"ways-to-define-probability\">Ways to define probability<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#ways-to-define-probability\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Definition</th><th>Formula</th><th>Idea</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Classical</strong> (equally likely)</td><td>P(E) = n(E) / n(S)</td><td>count favourable ÷ total outcomes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Relative frequency</strong></td><td>P(E) ≈ n(E) / N as N → ∞</td><td>long-run proportion of success</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Axiomatic (Kolmogorov)</strong></td><td>three axioms</td><td>see below</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Axioms:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><ol>\n<li>0 ≤ P(E) ≤ 1 for every event E.</li>\n<li>P(S) = 1 (something must happen).</li>\n<li>For mutually exclusive events: P(E1 ∪ E2 ∪ …) = P(E1) + P(E2) + …</li>\n</ol></blockquote>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Roll a fair die. P(even) = n{2,4,6}/6 = 3/6 = <strong>1/2</strong>. P(7) = 0 (impossible). P(≤6) = 1 (certain).</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"complement-rule\">Complement rule<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#complement-rule\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>P(E′) = 1 − P(E), where E′ = \"not E\". <em>Useful when \"not\" is easier to count.</em></p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>P(\"at least one head\" in 3 tosses) = 1 − P(no head) = 1 − (1/2)³ = 7/8.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"additive-law-of-probability\">Additive Law of Probability<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#additive-law-of-probability\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>For <strong>any two events A and B:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B) </div>\n<p>If <strong>A and B are mutually exclusive</strong> (A ∩ B = ∅), the law reduces to:</p>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) </div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>P(A) = 0.5, P(B) = 0.4, P(A ∩ B) = 0.2 → P(A ∪ B) = 0.5 + 0.4 − 0.2 = <strong>0.7</strong>. If instead A and B cannot occur together: P(A ∪ B) = 0.5 + 0.4 = <strong>0.9</strong>.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"three-events\">Three events<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#three-events\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>P(A ∪ B ∪ C) = P(A) + P(B) + P(C) − P(A∩B) − P(B∩C) − P(A∩C) + P(A∩B∩C)</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The additive law is your <strong>first tool</strong> for \"at least one\" and \"or\" questions. Subtract the overlap once so it is not counted twice.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"conditional-probability\">Conditional Probability<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#conditional-probability\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The probability of <strong>A given that B has occurred:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> P(A | B) = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"num\">P(A ∩ B)</span><span class=\"den\">P(B)</span></span> ,&nbsp; P(B) &gt; 0 </div>\n<p>Equivalently: <strong>P(A ∩ B) = P(B) · P(A | B)</strong> (the <em>multiplication rule</em>).</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>In a class of 60 students, 24 like cricket (C) and 18 of those also like football (F). P(F | C) = P(F ∩ C) / P(C) = (18/60) / (24/60) = 18/24 = <strong>3/4</strong>.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>P(A|B) ≠ P(B|A) in general. \"Chance you have the disease given a positive test\" is <em>not</em> the same as \"chance of a positive test given the disease.\"</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"independent-events\">Independent Events<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#independent-events\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Events A and B are <mark class=\"kw\">independent</mark> if the occurrence of one does <strong>not</strong> change the probability of the other:</p>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> P(A ∩ B) = P(A) · P(B) &nbsp;&nbsp;⇔&nbsp;&nbsp; P(A | B) = P(A) </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Independent events</td><td>P(A ∩ B) = P(A)·P(B)</td></tr><tr><td>Mutually exclusive events</td><td>P(A ∩ B) = 0 (they are <em>never</em> independent unless one has P = 0)</td></tr><tr><td>Both A and B \"at least one\"</td><td>P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A)·P(B)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Toss a coin twice. A = {first is H}, B = {second is H}. P(A ∩ B) = P(HH) = 1/4 = (1/2)(1/2) ✔ → <strong>independent</strong>. But A = {first H}, C = {exactly one H}: P(A ∩ C) = P(HT) = 1/4, while P(A)P(C) = (1/2)(1/2) = 1/4 — equal again here; in general always verify with the formula.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"bayes-theorem\">Bayes' Theorem<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#bayes-theorem\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Bayes' theorem <strong>reverses conditional probabilities</strong>. If events A1, A2, …, An are mutually exclusive and exhaustive (they partition S):</p>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> P(Ak | B) = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"num\">P(Ak) · P(B | Ak)</span><span class=\"den\">Σ P(Aj) · P(B | Aj)</span></span> </div>\n<p>Where P(Aj) are the <strong>prior</strong> probabilities and P(Aj | B) are the <strong>posterior</strong> probabilities.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Two machines: A makes 60% of items (1% defective), B makes 40% (2% defective). An item is found defective (D). P(A|D) = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"num\">(0.6)(0.01)</span><span class=\"den\">(0.6)(0.01) + (0.4)(0.02)</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"num\">0.006</span><span class=\"den\">0.014</span></span> = <strong>3/7 ≈ 0.4286</strong>.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Bayes' theorem exam recipe: (1) write the partition events and priors, (2) write the conditional probabilities P(B|Aj), (3) build the denominator as the weighted sum, (4) put the required term on top.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"random-variables\">Random Variables<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#random-variables\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">random variable (RV)</mark> is a <strong>function that assigns a real number to each outcome</strong> of a random experiment. It is <mark class=\"kw\">discrete</mark> if it takes <strong>only countably many values</strong> (finite or countable set).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>RV</th><th>Experiment</th><th>Values X =</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>X = number of heads</td><td>toss 3 coins</td><td>0, 1, 2, 3</td></tr><tr><td>X = sum</td><td>roll two dice</td><td>2, 3, …, 12</td></tr><tr><td>X = demand per day</td><td>count orders</td><td>0, 1, 2, …</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"probability-distribution-and-pmf\">Probability distribution and PMF<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#probability-distribution-and-pmf\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The <mark class=\"kw\">probability mass function (PMF)</mark> maps each value to its probability:</p>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> p(x) = P(X = x),&nbsp; with &nbsp; p(x) ≥ 0 and Σ<sub>x</sub> p(x) = 1 </div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Number of heads in 3 tosses (X = 0,1,2,3):</p></div></aside>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>x</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>p(x)</td><td>1/8</td><td>3/8</td><td>3/8</td><td>1/8</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Check: 1/8 + 3/8 + 3/8 + 1/8 = <strong>1</strong> ✔</p>\n<h3 id=\"distribution-cumulative-function\">Distribution (cumulative) function<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#distribution-cumulative-function\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> F(x) = P(X ≤ x) = Σ<sub>t ≤ x</sub> p(t) </div>\n<p>For the example above:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>x</th><th>&lt;0</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>≥4</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>F(x)</td><td>0</td><td>1/8</td><td>4/8</td><td>7/8</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Properties: F is non-decreasing, 0 ≤ F ≤ 1, F(−∞) = 0, F(+∞) = 1, and P(a < X ≤ b) = F(b) − F(a).</p>\n<h3 id=\"expectation-mean\">Expectation (mean)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#expectation-mean\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> E(X) = μ = Σ x · p(x) </div>\n<h3 id=\"moments\">Moments<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#moments\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The <strong>r-th moment about the origin</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> μ′<sub>r</sub> = E(X<sup>r</sup>) = Σ x<sup>r</sup> · p(x) </div>\n<p>So E(X) = μ′1 and E(X²) = μ′2.</p>\n<h3 id=\"variance-and-standard-deviation\">Variance and standard deviation<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#variance-and-standard-deviation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> Var(X) = σ² = E(X²) − [E(X)]²&nbsp;=&nbsp; Σ x²p(x) − μ² </div>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> SD(X) = σ = √Var(X) </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>E(X)</td><td>centre / average value of X</td></tr><tr><td>E(X²)</td><td>second moment (used for variance)</td></tr><tr><td>Var(X)</td><td>average squared distance from the mean (spread)</td></tr><tr><td>σ</td><td>standard deviation — spread in the same units as X</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>X = number of heads in 3 tosses: p = (1/8, 3/8, 3/8, 1/8) for x = (0,1,2,3). E(X) = 0·1/8 + 1·3/8 + 2·3/8 + 3·1/8 = 12/8 = <strong>1.5</strong>. E(X²) = 0 + 3/8 + 12/8 + 9/8 = 24/8 = <strong>3</strong>. Var(X) = 3 − (1.5)² = <strong>0.75</strong>; &nbsp; σ = √0.75 = √3/2 ≈ <strong>0.866</strong>.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Always use the <strong>shortcut</strong> Var(X) = E(X²) − μ² — it needs only two sums. The \"definitional\" form Σ(x−μ)²p(x) is more work and more error-prone in exams.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"linearity-rules-handy\">Linearity rules (handy)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#linearity-rules-handy\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>E(aX + b) = a·E(X) + b</li>\n<li>Var(aX + b) = a²·Var(X)  (constant shift does not change spread)</li>\n<li>E(X + Y) = E(X) + E(Y) (always; independence not needed)</li>\n<li>For <strong>independent</strong> X, Y: Var(X + Y) = Var(X) + Var(Y)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"quick-revision\">Quick Revision<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-revision\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B) </div>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B)&nbsp;&nbsp;;&nbsp;&nbsp; independent ⇔ P(A ∩ B) = P(A)·P(B) </div>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> P(Ak | B) = P(Ak)·P(B | Ak) / Σ P(Aj)·P(B | Aj) </div>\n<div class=\"formula big\"> E(X) = Σ x p(x) &nbsp;;&nbsp; Var(X) = E(X²) − μ² &nbsp;;&nbsp; σ = √Var(X) </div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice Questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#practice-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Two dice are rolled. Find P(sum = 7) and P(sum ≥ 10).</li>\n<li>P(A) = 0.6, P(B) = 0.5, P(A ∪ B) = 0.8. Find P(A ∩ B) and P(A | B).</li>\n<li>A bag has 4 red and 6 blue balls. Two are drawn without replacement. Find P(second is red | first is red).</li>\n<li>Prove that if P(A) > 0 and P(B) > 0, mutually exclusive events cannot be independent.</li>\n<li>A diagnostic test is 98% accurate for sick people and 95% specific for healthy people; 2% of a population is sick. Given a positive test, find P(sick).</li>\n<li>A discrete RV has PMF p(x) = k·x for x = 1,2,3,4. Find k, then E(X), Var(X), SD.</li>\n<li>For a fair die, X = face value. Compute E(X), E(X²), Var(X).</li>\n<li>Let F(x) be a CDF. Show that P(a < X ≤ b) = F(b) − F(a) and explain monotonicity.</li>\n<li>X and Y are independent with Var(X) = 4, Var(Y) = 9. Find Var(2X − 3Y + 5).</li>\n<li>The probability that a student passes maths is 0.7 and physics 0.6, both 0.5. Find P(passes at least one) and P(passes maths given physics).</li>\n</ol>"}, {"id": "ob-u1", "title": "OB Unit 1 — Introduction to OB & Foundations of Individual Behaviour", "subject": "ob", "unit": 1, "description": "Concept and importance of organizational behaviour, the role of managers, challenges and opportunities; then the foundations of individual behaviour — biographical characteristics, abilities, values, attitudes and workforce diversity — with tables and charts.", "tags": "organizational behavior, managers, biographical characteristics, abilities, values, attitudes, workforce diversity", "date": "2026-02-07", "featured": 1, "words": 1025, "reading_min": 5, "views": 231, "body_html": "<h2 id=\"introduction-to-organizational-behaviour\">Introduction to Organizational Behaviour<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#introduction-to-organizational-behaviour\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Organizational Behaviour (OB)</mark> is the systematic study of <strong>what people do in organizations</strong> and how their <strong>behaviour affects organization performance</strong>. It draws on psychology, sociology, social psychology, anthropology and political science.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-def\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Definition</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>OB studies the actions and attitudes of people in organizations: individuals, groups, and the structure that holds them together, with the goal of applying that knowledge to make organizations work more effectively.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"three-levels-of-ob-analysis\">Three levels of OB analysis<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#three-levels-of-ob-analysis\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"flow\"> <div class=\"fnode\">Organization level — structure, culture, systems</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↑</div> <div class=\"fnode\">Group level — teams, leadership, conflict, communication</div> <div class=\"farrow\">↑</div> <div class=\"fnode fmain\">Individual level — personality, perception, attitudes, learning, motivation</div> </div>\n<h3 id=\"importance-of-ob\">Importance of OB<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#importance-of-ob\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Reason</th><th>What it gives</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Understanding &amp; predicting</strong></td><td>anticipate what people will do in given situations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Managing people effectively</strong></td><td>motivate, coordinate, resolve conflict</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Improving productivity</strong></td><td>aligned goals, engaged workforce</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Better decision making</strong></td><td>uses evidence, not intuition alone</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Human resource perspective</strong></td><td>people are the most valuable asset</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Change management</strong></td><td>helps organizations adapt to change</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Work-life balance &amp; well-being</strong></td><td>healthier, more committed employees</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"role-of-managers-in-ob\">Role of Managers in OB<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#role-of-managers-in-ob\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><strong>Managers</strong> get things done <strong>through other people</strong>. Their four functions:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Planning</strong></td><td>decide goals and how to achieve them</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Organizing</strong></td><td>arrange tasks, people and resources</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Leading</strong></td><td>direct, motivate, communicate</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Controlling</strong></td><td>monitor progress and correct deviations</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Mintzberg's managerial roles</strong> (three groups):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Interpersonal</strong> — figurehead, leader, liaison.</li>\n<li><strong>Informational</strong> — monitor, disseminator, spokesperson.</li>\n<li><strong>Decisional</strong> — entrepreneur, disturbance handler, resource allocator, negotiator.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Managers also need <strong>technical</strong> skills (job-specific), <strong>human</strong> skills (work with people) and <strong>conceptual</strong> skills (see the big picture). As managers rise, human and conceptual skills matter more.</p>\n<h2 id=\"challenges-and-opportunities-for-ob\">Challenges and Opportunities for OB<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#challenges-and-opportunities-for-ob\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Challenge / Opportunity</th><th>OB response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Globalization</td><td>manage culturally diverse, remote teams</td></tr><tr><td>Workforce diversity</td><td>respect differences in age, gender, culture, ability</td></tr><tr><td>Employee well-being &amp; work–life balance</td><td>flexible hours, wellness programs</td></tr><tr><td>Technology &amp; remote work</td><td>virtual teams, digital communication skills</td></tr><tr><td>Retention of talent</td><td>engagement, growth, fair rewards</td></tr><tr><td>Ethical behaviour</td><td>codes of conduct, transparency</td></tr><tr><td>Positive work environment</td><td>trust, psychological safety, feedback culture</td></tr><tr><td>Temporary/agile structures</td><td>adaptability, empowerment</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Note</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The \"opportunity\" side: these challenges let managers who understand OB build <strong>competitive advantage</strong> through people.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"foundation-of-individual-behaviour\">Foundation of Individual Behaviour<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#foundation-of-individual-behaviour\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"biographical-characteristics\">Biographical characteristics<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#biographical-characteristics\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Easily observable personal factors that influence behaviour — <strong>age, gender, marital status, tenure, education, religious/ethnic background</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Characteristic</th><th>Typical OB observations</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Age</strong></td><td>older workers: less turnover/absenteeism, lower accident rates; innovation often younger</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gender</strong></td><td>no consistent difference in job performance; issues are cultural</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Marital status</strong></td><td>married employees often lower turnover and absenteeism</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tenure</strong></td><td>longer tenure → lower turnover, higher satisfaction</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Education</strong></td><td>influences training readiness and job assignment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Religion / ethnicity</strong></td><td>shape values and attitudes; must be respected (diversity)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Biographical characteristics are the <em>demographics</em> of behaviour — useful for prediction, but OB is confident only when they are combined with deeper factors like ability and personality.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"abilities\">Abilities<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#abilities\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Ability</mark> is the capacity to perform tasks — the physical and mental capabilities a person brings to a job.</p>\n<h4 id=\"types-of-ability\">Types of ability<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#types-of-ability\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h4>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Components</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Intellectual abilities</strong></td><td>number aptitude, verbal comprehension, perceptual speed, inductive/deductive reasoning, spatial visualization, memory</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Physical abilities</strong></td><td>strength (dynamic, trunk, static, explosive), flexibility, other factors (coordination, balance, stamina)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Ability–job <strong>fit</strong> matters: match skills to demands, else performance and satisfaction drop.</li>\n<li>Intellectual ability is central to most professional/technical jobs (measured by IQ-style tests); physical ability for athletes, technicians, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"values\">Values<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#values\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Values</mark> are <em>basic convictions</em> about what is personally/ socially preferable — they influence attitudes, motivation and decisions.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Content</strong></td><td><em>what</em> is important (freedom, security, achievement)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Intensity</strong></td><td><em>how important</em> it is</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Rokeach value survey</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Terminal values</strong> — desirable end-states (prosperity, peace, happiness, freedom).</li>\n<li><strong>Instrumental values</strong> — preferred <em>means</em> to ends (honesty, ambition, courage, helpfulness).</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Note</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Values are relatively <strong>stable and long-lasting</strong>, formed early through family, culture and schooling. Attitudes are more changeable — hence the effort to improve attitudes at work.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"attitudes\">Attitudes<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#attitudes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Attitude</mark> is a <em>evaluative statement</em> — \"favourable or unfavourable feeling\" toward a person, object or event.</p>\n<h4 id=\"three-components-of-attitude\">Three components of attitude<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#three-components-of-attitude\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h4>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cognitive</strong></td><td>belief/thought</td><td>\"My boss is fair\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Affective</strong></td><td>feeling/emotion</td><td>\"I like my workplace\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Behavioural</strong></td><td>intention to act</td><td>\"I will stay and work harder\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 id=\"types-key-attitudes-in-ob\">Types / key attitudes in OB<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#types-key-attitudes-in-ob\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h4>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Job attitude</th><th>About</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Job satisfaction</strong></td><td>positive feeling about one's job</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Job involvement</strong></td><td>psychological identification with the job</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Organizational commitment</strong></td><td>loyalty to, and identification with, the organization</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Perceived organizational support (POS)</strong></td><td>belief that the organization values you</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Cognitive dissonance</strong> — when attitudes and behaviour conflict (smoking despite knowing the risks), people feel tension and try to reduce it (change attitude, behaviour, or rationalize).</p>\n<h3 id=\"attitude-and-workforce-diversity\">Attitude and Workforce Diversity<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#attitude-and-workforce-diversity\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Diversity</mark> refers to differences among people: age, gender, race, ethnicity, disability, religion, education, culture, personality.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Benefit of diversity</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Broader ideas and creativity</td><td>inclusive culture</td></tr><tr><td>Better problem solving</td><td>psychological safety to speak up</td></tr><tr><td>Better market understanding</td><td>mirror diverse customers</td></tr><tr><td>Higher talent pool</td><td>unbiased hiring</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Attitudes toward diversity</strong> matter: positive, respectful attitudes convert diversity into performance; prejudice and stereotypes destroy it. Managers use <strong>diversity training</strong>, fair policies and inclusive leadership to shape attitudes.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A team with members from different cultures: attitudes of openness (cognitive: \"different views help\"; affective: interest; behavioural: inviting input) turn variety into better decisions. Stereotyped attitudes produce conflict and groupthink.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"quick-revision\">Quick Revision<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-revision\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>One-liner</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OB</td><td>study of what people do in organizations</td></tr><tr><td>3 levels</td><td>individual → group → organization</td></tr><tr><td>Manager functions</td><td>planning, organizing, leading, controlling</td></tr><tr><td>Mintzberg roles</td><td>interpersonal, informational, decisional</td></tr><tr><td>Biographical characteristics</td><td>age, gender, tenure, education… demographics</td></tr><tr><td>Abilities</td><td>intellectual + physical capabilities</td></tr><tr><td>Values</td><td>stable convictions; terminal vs instrumental</td></tr><tr><td>Attitude</td><td>cognitive + affective + behavioural components</td></tr><tr><td>Dissonance</td><td>tension when attitude and behaviour clash</td></tr><tr><td>Diversity</td><td>differences that must be respected and leveraged</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice Questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#practice-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Define OB. Explain its importance with three examples.</li>\n<li>What are the functions of a manager? Map them to a real work situation.</li>\n<li>List Mintzberg's ten roles grouped into three categories.</li>\n<li>Explain any four challenges or opportunities facing OB today.</li>\n<li>Comment on the relation of age and tenure with turnover and satisfaction.</li>\n<li>Differentiate intellectual from physical abilities with examples.</li>\n<li>Explain terminal and instrumental values using Rokeach's framework.</li>\n<li>Describe the three components of an attitude with a workplace example.</li>\n<li>Who creates a \"positive\" or \"toxic\" attitude toward diversity — and how?</li>\n<li>Using the ABC model, show how a manager can improve a negative job attitude.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"id": "ob-u2", "title": "OB Unit 2 — Personality, Emotions, Perception & Leadership", "subject": "ob", "unit": 2, "description": "Personality — definition, determinants and traits influencing OB; emotions — nature, dimensions and emotional intelligence; perception and its influencing factors; rational decision making and bounded rationality; and the trait, behavioural, situational and emerging approaches to leadership.", "tags": "personality, big five, emotions, emotional intelligence, perception, decision making, bounded rationality, leadership", "date": "2026-02-16", "featured": 1, "words": 1232, "reading_min": 6, "views": 208, "body_html": "<h2 id=\"personality\">Personality<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#personality\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Personality</mark> is the <strong>dynamic, organized set of characteristics</strong> that influences a person's thoughts, emotions, motivations and behaviour across situations. It is relatively stable but not rigidly fixed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"determinants-of-personality\">Determinants of personality<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#determinants-of-personality\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Determinant</th><th>Contribution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Heredity (biology)</strong></td><td>genes, physical build, temperament — the raw material</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Environment</strong></td><td>family, culture, schooling, peers, life experiences — shapes and modifies</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Situation</strong></td><td>the <em>current</em> context influences how personality is expressed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Note</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><strong>Heredity</strong> sets the foundation (twin studies show strong heritability of traits); <strong>environment</strong> (culture, family, experience) develops it; <strong>situation</strong> decides which traits <em>show</em> right now — an extrovert can be quiet in a formal meeting.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"personality-traits-influencing-ob\">Personality traits influencing OB<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#personality-traits-influencing-ob\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The <strong>Big Five (OCEAN)</strong> model is the most accepted trait framework:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Trait</th><th>High scorers tend to</th><th>OB relevance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Openness</strong></td><td>imaginative, curious, broad interests</td><td>creativity, adaptability</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Conscientiousness</strong></td><td>organized, dependable, disciplined</td><td>highest predictor of job performance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Extraversion</strong></td><td>sociable, assertive, energetic</td><td>sales, leadership, teamwork</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Agreeableness</strong></td><td>cooperative, trusting, friendly</td><td>teamwork, service roles</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Neuroticism (emotional stability)</strong></td><td>anxious, moody (low = calm)</td><td>resilience, stress management</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Other relevant traits: <strong>locus of control</strong> (internal vs external), <strong>self-esteem</strong>, <strong>self-monitoring</strong>, <strong>risk propensity</strong>, <strong>Type A/B</strong> behaviour, and <strong>Machiavellianism</strong> (ethics implications).</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><strong>Personality–job fit matters.</strong> Conscientiousness predicts performance in most jobs; extraversion suits social/sales roles; openness suits creative roles. A \"great trait\" in the wrong job still underperforms.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"emotions\">Emotions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#emotions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Emotions</mark> are <strong>intense, short-lived feelings</strong> directed at someone or something (anger at a colleague, joy at an award). They differ from <strong>moods</strong>, which are less intense and longer-lasting with no clear cause.</p>\n<h3 id=\"nature-of-emotions\">Nature of emotions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#nature-of-emotions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Biological</strong>: linked to physiological arousal (heart rate, hormones).</li>\n<li><strong>Universal</strong>: basic emotions (happiness, sadness, anger, fear, disgust, surprise) are recognized across cultures.</li>\n<li><strong>Cognitive evaluation</strong> triggers them: same event → different emotions for different people.</li>\n<li><strong>Visible</strong>: expressed through facial expressions and body language.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"emotional-dimensions\">Emotional dimensions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#emotional-dimensions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Valence</strong></td><td>pleasantness — positive (joy) vs negative (fear)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Intensity / arousal</strong></td><td>strength of the feeling — calm → excited</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Frequency/duration</strong></td><td>how often and how long emotions occur</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Emotional labour</strong> — employees (e.g., hospitality, nursing) must display organizationally required emotions even when they feel otherwise; <strong>emotional dissonance</strong> results when felt and displayed emotions differ.</p>\n<h3 id=\"emotional-intelligence-ei\">Emotional Intelligence (EI)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#emotional-intelligence-ei\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Emotional Intelligence</mark> is the ability to <strong>perceive, understand, use and manage</strong> one's own and others' emotions.</p>\n<p>Goleman's model (four/five competencies):</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Competency</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Self-awareness</strong></td><td>knowing your own emotions in real time</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Self-management</strong></td><td>regulating emotions, staying calm, adapting</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Social awareness (empathy)</strong></td><td>sensing others' feelings and needs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Relationship management</strong></td><td>influencing, inspiring, resolving conflict</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>EI predicts success in jobs with high interpersonal demands (managers, leaders, customer service) — often better than IQ alone. It can be developed with practice (feedback, mindfulness, empathy training).</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"perception-and-individual-decision-making\">Perception and Individual Decision Making<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#perception-and-individual-decision-making\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Perception</mark> is the process by which individuals <strong>organize and interpret</strong> sensory information to give meaning to their environment. Two people see the <em>same</em> event differently.</p>\n<h3 id=\"factors-influencing-perception\">Factors influencing perception<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#factors-influencing-perception\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Factor group</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Perceiver</strong></td><td>attitudes, motives, interests, past experience, expectations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Target</strong></td><td>novelty, motion, sound, size, background, proximity, similarity</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Situation</strong></td><td>time, work setting, social context</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Perceptual shortcuts (errors)</strong>: stereotyping, halo effect (one good trait colours everything), contrast effect, projection, selective perception, first-impression bias.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Two supervisors, same employee performance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Supervisor A</strong> (positive past experience with the employee)</td><td>sees diligent effort</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Supervisor B</strong> (recently burned by an employee who <code>looked similar</code>)</td><td>sees careless work — selectivity/stereotyping at work</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"rational-decision-making\">Rational Decision Making<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#rational-decision-making\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The <mark class=\"kw\">rational decision-making model</mark> assumes the decision maker is perfectly logical:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Activity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Define the problem</td><td>clear, complete statement of the issue</td></tr><tr><td>2. Identify decision criteria</td><td>weights for what matters</td></tr><tr><td>3. Allocate weights</td><td>relative importance of criteria</td></tr><tr><td>4. Develop alternatives</td><td>all feasible options</td></tr><tr><td>5. Evaluate alternatives</td><td>score each on the criteria</td></tr><tr><td>6. Select the best</td><td>highest total score</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Assumptions</strong>: complete information, all alternatives known, clear preferences, no time/cost constraints, maximization of outcomes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"bounded-rationality\">Bounded Rationality<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#bounded-rationality\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Bounded rationality</mark> (Simon) accepts that real decisions are made with <strong>limited information, limited time, and limited cognitive capacity</strong>. The decision maker:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Satisfices</strong> — chooses the first acceptable option, not the optimal one.</li>\n<li>Uses <strong>heuristics</strong> (rules of thumb) and simplifies the problem.</li>\n<li>Operates within the limits of their \"rationality\".</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Rational model</th><th>Bounded rationality</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Information</td><td>complete</td><td>limited</td></tr><tr><td>Alternatives</td><td>all known</td><td>a few found</td></tr><tr><td>Criteria</td><td>weighted formally</td><td>simplified</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome</td><td>maximize</td><td><strong>satisfice</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Reality</td><td>ideal, rarely used</td><td>describes actual decisions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"leadership-approaches\">Leadership — Approaches<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#leadership-approaches\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Leadership</mark> is the ability to <strong>influence a group</strong> toward the achievement of goals. It is a <em>process</em> (not a position) and involves followers, influence and goals.</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-trait-approaches\">1. Trait approaches<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#1-trait-approaches\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Early theories: leaders are <strong>born</strong> with distinguishing traits. Key traits found to matter:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Trait</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Drive</strong></td><td>ambition, energy, persistence</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Self-confidence</strong></td><td>belief in own ability</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Integrity</strong></td><td>honesty, trustworthiness</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Emotional intelligence</strong></td><td>self &amp; social awareness</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cognitive ability</strong></td><td>intelligence, analytical skill</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Extraversion</strong></td><td>sociability, assertiveness</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Traits alone <strong>cannot predict</strong> leadership — the research was inconclusive, so the field moved to <em>behaviours</em> and <em>situations</em>.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"2-behavioural-approaches\">2. Behavioural approaches<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#2-behavioural-approaches\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Leaders are <strong>made</strong> — leadership is learned behaviour. Two classic dimensions: <strong>task-oriented</strong> (structure, planning, output) and <strong>people-oriented</strong> (support, relationship, trust).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Study</th><th>Task orientation</th><th>People orientation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ohio State</td><td>Initiating structure</td><td>Consideration</td></tr><tr><td>Michigan</td><td>Production-centred</td><td>Employee-centred</td></tr><tr><td>Blake–Mouton managerial grid</td><td>Concern for production</td><td>Concern for people</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The <strong>Managerial Grid</strong> plots both concerns 1–9: <strong>Team management (9,9)</strong> — high on both — was the ideal; (1,1) impoverished; (9,1) authority–compliance; (1,9) country-club.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-situational-contingency-approaches\">3. Situational (contingency) approaches<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#3-situational-contingency-approaches\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>\"No one best style\" — effective leadership depends on the <strong>situation</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Core idea</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Fiedler's LPC</strong></td><td>match leader orientation (task vs relationship) to situation favourableness (leader–member relations, task structure, position power)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hersey–Blanchard SLT</strong></td><td>adapt style (telling, selling, participating, delegating) to follower <strong>readiness</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Path–Goal</strong></td><td>leader clarifies paths and removes obstacles to follower goals; style suits the situation (directive, supportive, participative, achievement)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>House's style flexibility</strong></td><td>same leader can use different styles</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"4-emerging-approaches\">4. Emerging approaches<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#4-emerging-approaches\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Key idea</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Transformational</strong></td><td>inspires followers to exceed expectations, changes the culture</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Transactional</strong></td><td>exchanges rewards for performance (contingent reward, management by exception)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Charismatic</strong></td><td>leader's vision, confidence and personal magnetism create devotion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Servant</strong></td><td>leader puts followers' needs first</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Authentic</strong></td><td>leader is genuine, transparent, values-driven</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ethical / shared / digital</strong></td><td>values-centred, distributed teams, tech-enabled influence</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><strong>Transformational vs Transactional</strong>: a <em>transactional</em> leader says \"meet your target, get your bonus\"; a <em>transformational</em> leader creates a vision (\"we will be #1\") that makes people work beyond self-interest. Both are useful — transactional maintains, transformational changes.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"quick-revision\">Quick Revision<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-revision\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>One-liner</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Personality</td><td>stable characteristic patterns; heredity + environment + situation</td></tr><tr><td>Big Five</td><td>Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, Neuroticism</td></tr><tr><td>Emotions</td><td>intense short-lived feelings; dimensions = valence, arousal, duration</td></tr><tr><td>EI</td><td>perceive, understand, manage emotions (self + others)</td></tr><tr><td>Perception</td><td>interpreting sensory input; perceiver/target/situation factors</td></tr><tr><td>Rational model</td><td>6-step maximizing decision process (ideal)</td></tr><tr><td>Bounded rationality</td><td>satisfice with limited information/time</td></tr><tr><td>Leadership approaches</td><td>trait → behavioural → situational → emerging</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice Questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#practice-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Define personality and discuss its determinants with examples.</li>\n<li>Explain the Big Five traits and which predicts job performance best.</li>\n<li>Distinguish emotions from moods. Explain any two emotional dimensions.</li>\n<li>What is emotional intelligence? Describe Goleman's competencies.</li>\n<li>Define perception and list the factors influencing it (perceiver, target, situation).</li>\n<li>What perceptual errors distort judgements in performance appraisal?</li>\n<li>Enumerate the six steps of rational decision making. What are its assumptions?</li>\n<li>Explain bounded rationality and satisficing with a real-life example.</li>\n<li>Compare the trait, behavioural and situational approaches of leadership.</li>\n<li>Differentiate transactional from transformational leadership with examples.</li>\n<li>On the Blake–Mouton grid, where is \"team management\" and why is it ideal?</li>\n<li>Suggest the appropriate Hersey–Blanchard style for a new, inexperienced team vs an expert team.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"id": "oop-u1", "title": "OOP Unit 1 — C++ Fundamentals & Object Oriented Concepts", "subject": "oop", "unit": 1, "description": "Introduction to C++, the standard library, header files, namespaces, control flow, functions and recursion, then the core object oriented concepts — encapsulation, polymorphism, overloading, inheritance, abstract classes, access specifiers and class scope.", "tags": "c++, classes, encapsulation, polymorphism, inheritance, recursion", "date": "2026-02-10", "featured": 1, "words": 2974, "reading_min": 15, "views": 412, "body_html": "<h2 id=\"introduction-to-c\">Introduction to C++<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#introduction-to-c\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">C++</mark> is a general-purpose, middle-level, compiled programming language created by <strong>Bjarne Stroustrup</strong> at Bell Laboratories. It began in 1979 as <em>\"C with Classes\"</em> and was renamed <strong>C++</strong> in 1983 (the <code>++</code> is the increment operator).</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-def\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Definition</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>C++ is a <strong>statically typed</strong>, <strong>case sensitive</strong>, <strong>free-form</strong> language that combines the efficiency and low-level control of C with high-level object oriented features such as classes, inheritance and polymorphism.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"why-c-is-called-a-middle-level-language\">Why C++ is called a middle-level language<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#why-c-is-called-a-middle-level-language\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Meaning</th><th>C++ support</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Low level</td><td>Direct hardware/memory access</td><td>pointers, bitwise operators, <code>new</code>/<code>delete</code></td></tr><tr><td>High level</td><td>Abstraction, reuse, safety</td><td>classes, templates, exceptions, STL</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"key-features\">Key features<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#key-features\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Object oriented</strong> — classes, objects, inheritance, polymorphism, encapsulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Compiled &amp; portable</strong> — source is compiled to machine code; the same program runs on many platforms after recompiling.</li>\n<li><strong>Rich standard library</strong> — strings, containers, algorithms, streams, files.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast</strong> — no garbage collector; you control memory.</li>\n<li><strong>Supports both procedural and OO styles</strong> — hence \"multi-paradigm\".</li>\n<li><strong>Operator overloading</strong> and <strong>function overloading</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exception handling</strong> with <code>try</code>, <code>catch</code>, <code>throw</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Templates</strong> for generic programming.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"c-vs-c\">C vs C++<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#c-vs-c\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>C</th><th>C++</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Paradigm</td><td>Procedural</td><td>Procedural + Object oriented</td></tr><tr><td>Data security</td><td>Low (global data)</td><td>High (encapsulation)</td></tr><tr><td>Functions</td><td>Top-level only</td><td>Member functions and top-level</td></tr><tr><td>Overloading</td><td>Not allowed</td><td>Allowed</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td><code>malloc</code>/<code>free</code></td><td><code>new</code>/<code>delete</code></td></tr><tr><td>Reference variables</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Namespace</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"applications-of-c\">Applications of C++<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#applications-of-c\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Operating systems and device drivers</li>\n<li>Games and game engines (Unreal Engine)</li>\n<li>Embedded and real-time systems</li>\n<li>Databases (MySQL, MongoDB internals)</li>\n<li>Browsers (Chrome, Firefox components)</li>\n<li>Compilers and interpreters</li>\n<li>Banking, telecom and simulation software</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"structure-of-a-simple-c-program\">Structure of a Simple C++ Program<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#structure-of-a-simple-c-program\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">// first.cpp - the smallest useful C++ program\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;          // 1. header file for input/output\nusing namespace std;         // 2. bring std names into scope\n\nint main()                   // 3. execution always starts at main()\n{\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"Hello, World!\" &lt;&lt; endl;   // 4. statement ends with ;\n    return 0;                // 5. 0 means success\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Every C++ program is a collection of one or more <strong>functions</strong>, and execution begins at <code>main()</code>. Statements end with a semicolon (<code>;</code>). C++ is <strong>case sensitive</strong>, so <code>Main</code>, <code>main</code> and <code>MAIN</code> are different.</p>\n<h3 id=\"comments\">Comments<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#comments\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">// single line comment  (C++ style)\n/* multi-line\n   comment (C style) */</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"the-c-standard-library\">The C++ Standard Library<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#the-c-standard-library\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The <mark class=\"kw\">standard library</mark> is a set of pre-written classes and functions shipped with every C++ compiler. Its most used parts are the <strong>iostream</strong> library for I/O and the <strong>STL</strong> (Standard Template Library) for containers and algorithms.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Header</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Common names</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>&lt;iostream&gt;</code></td><td>console input/output</td><td><code>cin</code>, <code>cout</code>, <code>cerr</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;iomanip&gt;</code></td><td>output formatting</td><td><code>setw</code>, <code>setprecision</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;string&gt;</code></td><td><code>std::string</code> class</td><td><code>string</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;vector&gt;</code></td><td>dynamic array</td><td><code>vector</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;array&gt;</code></td><td>fixed-size array wrapper</td><td><code>array</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;algorithm&gt;</code></td><td>sorting/searching</td><td><code>sort</code>, <code>find</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;map&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;set&gt;</code></td><td>associative containers</td><td><code>map</code>, <code>set</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;cmath&gt;</code></td><td>math functions</td><td><code>sqrt</code>, <code>pow</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;fstream&gt;</code></td><td>file I/O</td><td><code>ifstream</code>, <code>ofstream</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;cstdlib&gt;</code></td><td>general utilities</td><td><code>atoi</code>, <code>rand</code>, <code>exit</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; marks = {72, 90, 65, 88};\n    sort(marks.begin(), marks.end());     // ascending\n    for (int m : marks) cout &lt;&lt; m &lt;&lt; \" \"; // 65 72 88 90\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\nTotal: \";\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int m : marks) sum += m;\n    cout &lt;&lt; sum &lt;&lt; endl;\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Prefer <code>std::cout</code> over <code>using namespace std;</code> in large projects, because <code>using namespace std;</code> can cause name clashes. For small programs and exams it is perfectly fine.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"header-files\">Header Files<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#header-files\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">header file</mark> contains declarations (function prototypes, class definitions, macros, constants) that can be shared by many <code>.cpp</code> files.</p>\n<h3 id=\"two-kinds-of-headers\">Two kinds of headers<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#two-kinds-of-headers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Included with</th><th>Example</th><th>Search path</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Standard (system)</td><td><code>#include &lt;name&gt;</code></td><td><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;</code></td><td>compiler's include directories</td></tr><tr><td>User-defined</td><td><code>#include \"name.h\"</code></td><td><code>#include \"student.h\"</code></td><td>current directory first</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-headers-are-used\">Why headers are used<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#why-headers-are-used\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reuse</strong> — write once, include anywhere.</li>\n<li><strong>Separation</strong> — interface (<code>.h</code>) stays separate from implementation (<code>.cpp</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Faster compilation</strong> — change a <code>.cpp</code> without recompiling everything.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"example-a-user-defined-header\">Example: a user-defined header<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#example-a-user-defined-header\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">// student.h\n#ifndef STUDENT_H            // include guard: prevents double inclusion\n#define STUDENT_H\n\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nclass Student {\npublic:\n    Student(std::string n, int m);\n    void display() const;\nprivate:\n    std::string name;\n    int marks;\n};\n\n#endif</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">// student.cpp\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include \"student.h\"\n\nStudent::Student(std::string n, int m) : name(n), marks(m) {}\n\nvoid Student::display() const {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; \" scored \" &lt;&lt; marks &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">// main.cpp\n#include \"student.h\"\n\nint main() {\n    Student s(\"Riya\", 91);\n    s.display();\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Without an include guard (<code>#ifndef</code>/<code>#define</code>/<code>#endif</code>) or <code>#pragma once</code>, a header included twice causes \"redefinition\" compiler errors.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"namespaces\">Namespaces<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#namespaces\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">namespace</mark> is a named region that groups declarations so that names in different libraries do not collide.</p>\n<h3 id=\"syntax\">Syntax<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#syntax\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">namespace Math {\n    const double PI = 3.14159;\n    double area(double r) { return PI * r * r; }\n}\n\nnamespace Physics {\n    const double PI = 3.14159;   // no clash: different namespace\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; Math::area(2) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";   // fully qualified name\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"using-declaration-vs-using-directive\">using-declaration vs using-directive<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#using-declaration-vs-using-directive\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>using-declaration</td><td><code>using std::cout;</code></td><td>brings <strong>one</strong> name into scope</td></tr><tr><td>using-directive</td><td><code>using namespace std;</code></td><td>brings <strong>all</strong> names of the namespace into scope</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"other-namespace-features\">Other namespace features<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#other-namespace-features\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nested namespaces:</strong> <code>namespace A { namespace B { int x; } }</code> &rarr; use <code>A::B::x</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Namespace alias:</strong> <code>namespace io = std::ios;</code></li>\n<li><strong>Anonymous namespace:</strong> names have internal linkage (like <code>static</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Global namespace:</strong> names declared outside every namespace; accessed with <code>::name</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Note</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><code>std</code> is the namespace of the C++ standard library. Everything in <code>&lt;iostream&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;string&gt;</code>, etc. lives inside <code>std</code>.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"control-flow\">Control Flow<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#control-flow\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"variables-and-assignment-statements\">Variables and assignment statements<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#variables-and-assignment-statements\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">variable</mark> is a named memory location that stores a value. Declaration reserves the memory; <strong>initialization</strong> gives it a first value; <strong>assignment</strong> changes it later.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int age = 18;        // declaration + initialization\ndouble pi;           // declaration only (garbage value!)\npi = 3.14159;        // assignment\nconst int MAX = 100; // constant: cannot be changed\nint a = 5, b = a + 3;</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"fundamental-data-types\">Fundamental data types<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#fundamental-data-types\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Typical size</th><th>Range (typical)</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>bool</code></td><td>1 byte</td><td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td><td><code>bool ok = true;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>char</code></td><td>1 byte</td><td>-128 to 127</td><td><code>char c = 'A';</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int</code></td><td>4 bytes</td><td>-2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647</td><td><code>int n = 42;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>float</code></td><td>4 bytes</td><td>~7 digits precision</td><td><code>float r = 2.5f;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>double</code></td><td>8 bytes</td><td>~15 digits precision</td><td><code>double d = 3.14;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>void</code></td><td>—</td><td>no value</td><td>function return type</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Modifiers: <code>short</code>, <code>long</code>, <code>signed</code>, <code>unsigned</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"conditional-execution\">Conditional execution<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#conditional-execution\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int marks = 76;\n\nif (marks &gt;= 90) {\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"Grade A\";\n} else if (marks &gt;= 75) {\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"Grade B\";\n} else if (marks &gt;= 60) {\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"Grade C\";\n} else {\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"Grade D\";\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<h4 id=\"switch-statement\">switch statement<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#switch-statement\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h4>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">char op = '+';\nswitch (op) {\n    case '+': cout &lt;&lt; \"Addition\"; break;\n    case '-': cout &lt;&lt; \"Subtraction\"; break;\n    default:  cout &lt;&lt; \"Unknown operator\";\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<h4 id=\"ternary-conditional-operator\">Ternary (conditional) operator<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#ternary-conditional-operator\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h4>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int a = 10, b = 20;\nint larger = (a &gt; b) ? a : b;   // 20</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"looping\">Looping<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#looping\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Loop</th><th>Use when</th><th>Test happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>for</code></td><td>number of iterations is known</td><td>before each iteration</td></tr><tr><td><code>while</code></td><td>repeats while a condition holds</td><td>before each iteration</td></tr><tr><td><code>do…while</code></td><td>body must run at least once</td><td>after each iteration</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) cout &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; \" \";    // 1 2 3 4 5\n\nint n = 5;\nwhile (n &gt; 0) { cout &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; \" \"; n--; }          // 5 4 3 2 1\n\nint k = 0;\ndo { cout &lt;&lt; k &lt;&lt; \" \"; k++; } while (k &lt; 3);      // 0 1 2\n\nfor (int x : {10, 20, 30}) cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; \" \";      // range-based for</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>break</code> exits the loop immediately; <code>continue</code> skips to the next iteration.</p>\n<h3 id=\"function-calls\">Function calls<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#function-calls\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }        // definition\n\nint main() {\n    int s = add(3, 4);                         // call by value\n    cout &lt;&lt; s;                                  // 7\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<h4 id=\"call-by-value-vs-call-by-reference\">Call by value vs call by reference<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#call-by-value-vs-call-by-reference\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h4>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">void byValue(int x) { x = 100; }      // original unchanged\nvoid byRef(int &amp;x)  { x = 100; }      // original changed\n\nint main() {\n    int n = 5;\n    byValue(n); cout &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; \" \";      // 5\n    byRef(n);   cout &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";     // 100\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<h4 id=\"function-overloading\">Function overloading<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#function-overloading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h4>\n<p>Two or more functions may share a name if their <strong>parameter lists differ</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int    area(int side)          { return side * side; }\ndouble area(double r)          { return 3.14159 * r * r; }\nint    area(int l, int b)      { return l * b; }</code></pre></figure>\n<h4 id=\"default-arguments\">Default arguments<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#default-arguments\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h4>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int power(int base, int exp = 2) {   // exp defaults to 2\n    int r = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; exp; i++) r *= base;\n    return r;\n}\n// power(5) -&gt; 25 ; power(2, 10) -&gt; 1024</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"recursion\">Recursion<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#recursion\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Recursion</mark> is a technique where a function calls itself on a smaller version of the same problem. Every recursive function needs a <strong>base case</strong> (to stop) and a <strong>recursive case</strong> (to make progress).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">// Factorial: n! = n * (n-1)!\nlong factorial(int n) {\n    if (n &lt;= 1) return 1;          // base case\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);   // recursive case\n}\n// factorial(5) = 5*4*3*2*1 = 120</code></pre></figure>\n<h4 id=\"how-the-calls-unwind-for-factorial-4\">How the calls unwind for <code>factorial(4)</code><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#how-the-calls-unwind-for-factorial-4\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h4>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">factorial(4)\n -&gt; 4 * factorial(3)\n        -&gt; 3 * factorial(2)\n               -&gt; 2 * factorial(1)\n                      -&gt; 1  (base case returns)\n               &lt;- 2 * 1 = 2\n        &lt;- 3 * 2 = 6\n &lt;- 4 * 6 = 24</code></pre></figure>\n<h4 id=\"fibonacci-series\">Fibonacci series<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#fibonacci-series\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h4>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">int fib(int n) {\n    if (n &lt;= 1) return n;\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\n}\n// 0 1 1 2 3 5 8 13 ...</code></pre></figure>\n<h4 id=\"tower-of-hanoi\">Tower of Hanoi<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#tower-of-hanoi\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h4>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">void hanoi(int n, char from, char to, char via) {\n    if (n == 1) { cout &lt;&lt; \"Move disk 1: \" &lt;&lt; from &lt;&lt; \" -&gt; \" &lt;&lt; to &lt;&lt; \"\\n\"; return; }\n    hanoi(n - 1, from, via, to);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"Move disk \" &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; \": \" &lt;&lt; from &lt;&lt; \" -&gt; \" &lt;&lt; to &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    hanoi(n - 1, via, to, from);\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Recursion vs iteration</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Recursion</th><th>Iteration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Memory</td><td>uses the call stack</td><td>uses a loop variable</td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>usually slower</td><td>usually faster</td></tr><tr><td>Code</td><td>shorter, closer to the maths</td><td>longer, but explicit</td></tr><tr><td>Risk</td><td>stack overflow if too deep</td><td>none</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"object-oriented-concepts\">Object Oriented Concepts<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#object-oriented-concepts\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">OOP</mark> organises a program around <strong>objects</strong> — self-contained units that bundle data and the functions that operate on that data — instead of around functions and global data.</p>\n<h3 id=\"objects-and-classes\">Objects and classes<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#objects-and-classes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A <mark class=\"kw\">class</mark> is a user-defined data type: a <strong>blueprint</strong> describing data members and member functions.</li>\n<li>An <mark class=\"kw\">object</mark> is a <strong>variable of a class</strong> — a real instance created from the blueprint.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Student {\nprivate:\n    string name;\n    int marks;\npublic:\n    void setData(string n, int m) { name = n; marks = m; }\n    void display() const { cout &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; \" : \" &lt;&lt; marks &lt;&lt; \"\\n\"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Student s1;                 // s1 is an object\n    s1.setData(\"Aman\", 84);\n    s1.display();\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"encapsulation\">Encapsulation<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#encapsulation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Encapsulation</mark> means wrapping data and the functions that manipulate it into a single unit (the class), and <strong>hiding</strong> the internal data from outside code. Data is made <code>private</code>; controlled access is provided through public member functions.</p>\n<p>Benefits: data security, modularity, easier maintenance.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class BankAccount {\nprivate:\n    double balance;                       // hidden\npublic:\n    BankAccount() : balance(0) {}\n    void deposit(double amt) { if (amt &gt; 0) balance += amt; }\n    double getBalance() const { return balance; }\n};</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"polymorphism\">Polymorphism<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#polymorphism\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Polymorphism</mark> (\"many forms\") lets the same name or interface behave differently in different situations.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>When resolved</th><th>Achieved by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Compile-time (static)</td><td>at compile time</td><td>function overloading, operator overloading, templates</td></tr><tr><td>Run-time (dynamic)</td><td>at run time</td><td>virtual functions + inheritance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Shape {\npublic:\n    virtual void draw() const { cout &lt;&lt; \"Some shape\\n\"; }\n};\n\nclass Circle : public Shape {\npublic:\n    void draw() const override { cout &lt;&lt; \"Circle\\n\"; }\n};\n\nclass Square : public Shape {\npublic:\n    void draw() const override { cout &lt;&lt; \"Square\\n\"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Shape* shapes[2];\n    shapes[0] = new Circle();\n    shapes[1] = new Square();\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 2; i++) shapes[i]-&gt;draw();   // Circle, Square\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Without the keyword <code>virtual</code>, the call <code>shapes[i]-&gt;draw()</code> would bind to <code>Shape::draw</code> at compile time — that is the whole point of run-time polymorphism.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"overloading\">Overloading<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#overloading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><strong>Function overloading</strong> — same name, different parameter list (shown earlier).</p>\n<p><strong>Operator overloading</strong> — give a new meaning to an existing operator for a user-defined type.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Complex {\n    double re, im;\npublic:\n    Complex(double r = 0, double i = 0) : re(r), im(i) {}\n    Complex operator+(const Complex&amp; c) const {\n        return Complex(re + c.re, im + c.im);\n    }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; re &lt;&lt; \" + \" &lt;&lt; im &lt;&lt; \"i\\n\"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Complex a(1, 2), b(3, 4);\n    Complex c = a + b;          // calls operator+\n    c.show();                   // 4 + 6i\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Rules: you cannot create new operators, cannot change precedence, and a few operators (<code>::</code>, <code>.</code>, <code>.*</code>, <code>?:</code>, <code>sizeof</code>) cannot be overloaded.</p>\n<h3 id=\"inheritance\">Inheritance<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#inheritance\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Inheritance</mark> lets a new class (<strong>derived</strong> or child) acquire the properties of an existing class (<strong>base</strong> or parent). It models an <em>is-a</em> relationship and promotes reuse.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Animal {                  // base\npublic:\n    void eat() const { cout &lt;&lt; \"eating\\n\"; }\n};\n\nclass Dog : public Animal {     // derived\npublic:\n    void bark() const { cout &lt;&lt; \"barking\\n\"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Dog d;\n    d.eat();     // inherited\n    d.bark();    // own\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Types of inheritance (covered in Unit 2): single, multiple, multilevel, hierarchical, hybrid.</p>\n<h3 id=\"abstract-classes\">Abstract classes<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#abstract-classes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>An <mark class=\"kw\">abstract class</mark> is a class that contains at least one <mark class=\"kw\">pure virtual function</mark> and therefore <strong>cannot be instantiated</strong>. It defines an interface for derived classes.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Shape {\npublic:\n    virtual double area() const = 0;   // pure virtual\n    virtual ~Shape() {}\n};\n\nclass Rectangle : public Shape {\n    double w, h;\npublic:\n    Rectangle(double w, double h) : w(w), h(h) {}\n    double area() const override { return w * h; }\n};</code></pre></figure>\n<p>A pure virtual function is declared by assigning <code>= 0</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"access-specifiers\">Access specifiers<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#access-specifiers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Specifier</th><th>Same class</th><th>Derived class</th><th>Outside code</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>private</code></td><td>yes</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><code>protected</code></td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><code>public</code></td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"class-scope-and-accessing-class-members\">Class scope and accessing class members<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#class-scope-and-accessing-class-members\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A class body defines a <strong>scope</strong>. Names declared in it are visible to all member functions. Outside the class, members are reached with the <strong>dot operator</strong> on an object, or the <strong>arrow operator</strong> on a pointer.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">Student s;\ns.display();          // dot on an object\nStudent* p = &amp;s;\np-&gt;display();         // arrow on a pointer</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Inside a member function you may refer to members directly; <code>this-&gt;member</code> is also valid. The <code>::</code> scope resolution operator links a member function defined outside the class:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">void Student::display() const { cout &lt;&lt; name; }</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"constant-class-members\">Constant class members<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#constant-class-members\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Circle {\n    const double pi;          // const data member\npublic:\n    Circle() : pi(3.14159) {} // must be initialised in the initialiser list\n    double area(double r) const { return pi * r * r; }  // const member function\n};</code></pre></figure>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>const data member</strong> cannot be changed after construction and must be initialised in the constructor's initialiser list.</li>\n<li>A <strong>const member function</strong> promises not to modify the object; it may be called on <code>const</code> objects.</li>\n<li>A <code>const</code> object can only call <code>const</code> member functions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"structure-vs-class\">Structure vs Class<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#structure-vs-class\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th><code>struct</code></th><th><code>class</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Default access</td><td><code>public</code></td><td><code>private</code></td></tr><tr><td>Default inheritance</td><td><code>public</code></td><td><code>private</code></td></tr><tr><td>Typical use</td><td>plain data grouping (POD)</td><td>full object with behaviour</td></tr><tr><td>Members</td><td>data and functions allowed</td><td>data and functions allowed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>In C++ the two are otherwise identical.</p>\n<h3 id=\"macro-vs-inline-function\">Macro vs Inline function<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#macro-vs-inline-function\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Macro (<code>#define</code>)</th><th>Inline function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Processed by</td><td>preprocessor (text replacement)</td><td>compiler</td></tr><tr><td>Type checking</td><td>none</td><td>full type checking</td></tr><tr><td>Debugging</td><td>hard</td><td>easy, behaves like a function</td></tr><tr><td>Side effects</td><td><code>SQR(x++)</code> increments twice</td><td>evaluated once</td></tr><tr><td>Syntax</td><td><code>#define SQR(x) ((x)*(x))</code></td><td><code>inline int sqr(int x){return x*x;}</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">#define SQR(x) ((x)*(x))              // macro\ninline int square(int x) { return x * x; }  // inline function</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><code>SQR(i++)</code> expands to <code>((i++)*(i++))</code>, which increments <code>i</code> twice. The inline function <code>square(i++)</code> increments once. Prefer inline functions.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"quick-revision\">Quick Revision<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-revision\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>One-line meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Encapsulation</td><td>bundle data + methods, hide data</td></tr><tr><td>Abstraction</td><td>show only essential features</td></tr><tr><td>Polymorphism</td><td>same interface, many behaviours</td></tr><tr><td>Overloading</td><td>same name, different parameters</td></tr><tr><td>Inheritance</td><td>derived class reuses base class</td></tr><tr><td>Abstract class</td><td>has a pure virtual function, cannot be instantiated</td></tr><tr><td><code>this</code></td><td>pointer to the current object (Unit 2)</td></tr><tr><td>Virtual function</td><td>enables run-time polymorphism</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><strong>Exam favourite:</strong> \"Differentiate compile-time and run-time polymorphism with one example each.\" Compile-time = function/operator overloading resolved by the compiler. Run-time = <code>virtual</code> functions resolved through a base-class pointer at run time.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice Questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#practice-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li>List any five features of C++ and explain any two.</li>\n<li>What is a namespace? Illustrate with a program using two namespaces that both define <code>PI</code>.</li>\n<li>Explain the difference between <code>#include &lt;file&gt;</code> and <code>#include \"file\"</code>.</li>\n<li>Write a recursive function to compute the sum of the first <code>n</code> natural numbers.</li>\n<li>Differentiate between function overloading and operator overloading with examples.</li>\n<li>Explain the four access specifiers with a suitable class example.</li>\n<li>Compare a macro with an inline function; give one program where the macro gives a wrong result.</li>\n<li>What is an abstract class? Why can it not be instantiated?</li>\n<li>Explain encapsulation and data hiding with a <code>BankAccount</code> example.</li>\n<li>Trace the output of <code>factorial(4)</code> and draw the call stack.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"id": "oop-u2", "title": "OOP Unit 2 — Friends, this, Memory, Constructors & Inheritance", "subject": "oop", "unit": 2, "description": "Friend functions and friend classes, the this pointer, new/delete, static members, constructors and destructors, and inheritance — types, overriding, access modes and base class constructor/destructor effects.", "tags": "c++, friend, this, new delete, static, constructors, destructors, inheritance", "date": "2026-02-12", "featured": 1, "words": 2481, "reading_min": 12, "views": 356, "body_html": "<h2 id=\"friend-function-and-friend-classes\">Friend Function and Friend Classes<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#friend-function-and-friend-classes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<h3 id=\"friend-function\">Friend function<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#friend-function\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">friend function</mark> is a <strong>non-member function</strong> that has permission to access the <code>private</code> and <code>protected</code> members of a class. It is declared inside the class with the keyword <code>friend</code> and defined outside.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Box {\nprivate:\n    double side;\npublic:\n    Box(double s) : side(s) {}\n    friend double volume(Box b);    // grants access\n};\n\ndouble volume(Box b) {              // not a member function!\n    return b.side * b.side * b.side;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Box b(3);\n    cout &lt;&lt; volume(b) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";      // 27\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Key rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Declared with <code>friend</code> inside the class; <strong>defined outside</strong> without <code>friend</code> or <code>Box::</code>.</li>\n<li>It is <strong>not</strong> a member function, so it has no <code>this</code> pointer and cannot be invoked with a dot.</li>\n<li>Friendship is <strong>granted by the class, not taken</strong> — it is not mutual and not inherited.</li>\n<li>A friend function can be made a friend of <strong>two classes</strong> at once (handy for operators like <code>+</code> between two types).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"friend-of-two-classes\">Friend of two classes<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#friend-of-two-classes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h4>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class B;                                  // forward declaration\n\nclass A {\n    int x;\npublic:\n    A(int v) : x(v) {}\n    friend int sum(A, B);                 // friend of A\n};\n\nclass B {\n    int y;\npublic:\n    B(int v) : y(v) {}\n    friend int sum(A, B);                 // friend of B\n};\n\nint sum(A a, B b) { return a.x + b.y; }</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"friend-class\">Friend class<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#friend-class\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>If class <code>B</code> is declared a <strong>friend class</strong> of class <code>A</code>, <strong>every member function of B</strong> can access the private members of <code>A</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class B;                                  // forward declaration\n\nclass A {\n    int secret;\npublic:\n    A() : secret(42) {}\n    friend class B;                       // B can peek into A\n};\n\nclass B {\npublic:\n    int reveal(A a) { return a.secret; }  // legal now\n};</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Note</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Friend classes should be used sparingly; they weaken encapsulation. They are useful when two classes are tightly coupled, e.g. a <code>List</code> and its <code>Node</code>.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"the-this-pointer\">The this Pointer<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#the-this-pointer\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Every non-static member function receives a hidden pointer <mark class=\"kw\"><code>this</code></mark> that points to the <strong>object on which the function was called</strong>. Inside a member function, <code>this-&gt;member</code> and <code>member</code> are equivalent.</p>\n<p>Uses of <code>this</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Resolve <strong>name shadowing</strong> (parameter has the same name as a member).</li>\n<li><strong>Chain</strong> method calls (<code>return *this;</code>).</li>\n<li>Access the object's <strong>address</strong> (<code>return this;</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Counter {\n    int count;\npublic:\n    Counter() : count(0) {}\n    Counter&amp; set(int count) {     // parameter shadows member\n        this-&gt;count = count;      // disambiguation\n        return *this;             // enables chaining\n    }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; count &lt;&lt; \"\\n\"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Counter c;\n    c.set(5).set(9);   // chaining\n    c.show();          // 9\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><code>this</code> is a <strong>const pointer</strong> — you can change the object it points to but not make <code>this</code> point elsewhere. Static member functions do <strong>not</strong> have a <code>this</code> pointer.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"dynamic-memory-allocation-and-deallocation\">Dynamic Memory Allocation and Deallocation<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#dynamic-memory-allocation-and-deallocation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Static allocation (local variables, arrays) happens at compile time on the <strong>stack</strong>. Dynamic allocation happens at <strong>run time</strong> on the <strong>heap (free store)</strong> using <code>new</code> and <code>delete</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>new &lt;type&gt;</code></td><td>allocate one object</td><td><code>int *p = new int;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>new &lt;type&gt;(value)</code></td><td>allocate + initialise</td><td><code>int *p = new int(7);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>new &lt;type&gt;[n]</code></td><td>allocate an array</td><td><code>int *a = new int[10];</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>delete &lt;ptr&gt;</code></td><td>free one object</td><td><code>delete p;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>delete[] &lt;ptr&gt;</code></td><td>free an array</td><td><code>delete[] a;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>new</code> on a class</td><td>allocates + calls constructor</td><td><code>Box *b = new Box(3);</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    int *p = new int(21);        // one int, value 21\n    cout &lt;&lt; *p &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    delete p;                    // free it\n\n    int n = 5;\n    int *arr = new int[n];       // dynamic array\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) arr[i] = i * i;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) cout &lt;&lt; arr[i] &lt;&lt; \" \";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    delete[] arr;                // free the array (delete[], not delete)\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Every <code>new</code> must be matched by a <code>delete</code>; every <code>new[]</code> must be matched by <code>delete[]</code>. Leaks happen when allocated memory is never freed; a mismatch (using <code>delete</code> on an array) is undefined behaviour.</p></div></aside>\n<p><strong>Why use <code>new</code>/<code>delete</code> instead of <code>malloc</code>/<code>free</code>?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>new</code> calls the <strong>constructor</strong>; <code>delete</code> calls the <strong>destructor</strong>.</li>\n<li><code>new</code> returns the correct typed pointer (no <code>malloc</code> casts).</li>\n<li>Operators can be overloaded for a class to control allocation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"static-class-members\">Static Class Members<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#static-class-members\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>static</code> members <strong>belong to the class</strong>, not to any single object. There is exactly one copy shared by all objects, and they exist even before any object is created.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Student {\n    string name;\n    static int total;                 // declaration (counts students)\npublic:\n    Student(string n) : name(n) { total++; }\n    static int count() { return total; }\n};\n\nint Student::total = 0;               // definition &amp; initialisation\n\nint main() {\n    Student a(\"Aman\"), b(\"Riya\"), c(\"Dev\");\n    cout &lt;&lt; Student::count() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\"; // 3  (called on the class)\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Static data member</th><th>Static member function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Owned by</td><td>the class (one copy)</td><td>the class</td></tr><tr><td>Access</td><td><code>Class::member</code> or via any object</td><td><code>Class::function()</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>this</code> pointer</td><td>—</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>Can access non-static members</td><td>no</td><td>no (no object context)</td></tr><tr><td>Must be defined outside the class (in C++ before C++17)</td><td>yes</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><code>static</code> data members must be <strong>defined outside the class</strong> (<code>int Student::total = 0;</code>) because they need storage of their own. (Since C++17 you can write <code>inline static int total = 0;</code>.)</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"constructors\">Constructors<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#constructors\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">constructor</mark> is a special member function that is <strong>automatically called when an object is created</strong>. It has the same name as the class, no return type (not even <code>void</code>), and may be overloaded.</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-default-constructor\">1. Default constructor<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#1-default-constructor\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A constructor with <strong>no parameters</strong> (automatically supplied by the compiler if no constructor is written).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Point {\n    int x, y;\npublic:\n    Point() { x = 0; y = 0; }   // default constructor\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; \"(\" &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; \",\" &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; \")\\n\"; }\n};</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"2-parameterized-constructor\">2. Parameterized constructor<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#2-parameterized-constructor\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Takes arguments so an object can be created with initial values.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Point {\n    int x, y;\npublic:\n    Point(int a, int b) { x = a; y = b; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; \"(\" &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; \",\" &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; \")\\n\"; }\n};\n\nPoint p1(3, 5);        // implicit call\nPoint p2 = Point(1, 2);// explicit call</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Constructor initialiser list</strong> – preferred because it initialises (does not assign) members:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Point {\n    int x, y;\npublic:\n    Point(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) {}   // initialiser list\n};</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"3-copy-constructor\">3. Copy constructor<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#3-copy-constructor\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>Creates a new object as a <strong>copy of an existing object</strong>. The compiler provides a default (shallow) copy constructor.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Point {\n    int x, y;\npublic:\n    Point(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) {}\n    Point(const Point &amp;p) {       // copy constructor\n        x = p.x;\n        y = p.y;\n    }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; \"(\" &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; \",\" &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; \")\\n\"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Point p1(3, 5);\n    Point p2 = p1;        // copy constructor invoked\n    Point p3(p1);         // another way\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><strong>Shallow vs deep copy.</strong> If a class owns memory via a pointer, the default copy copies only the <em>pointer</em> (shallow) — two objects then share the same memory and <code>delete</code> twice will crash. A user-defined copy constructor performing <strong>deep copy</strong> allocates its own memory.</p></div></aside>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class IntArr {\n    int *data;\n    int n;\npublic:\n    IntArr(int size) : n(size)\n        { data = new int[n]; }\n    IntArr(const IntArr &amp;src) : n(src.n) {      // deep copy\n        data = new int[n];\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) data[i] = src.data[i];\n    }\n    ~IntArr() { delete[] data; }\n};\n\nIntArr b = a;   // b gets its own copy of the array</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"when-is-a-copy-constructor-called\">When is a copy constructor called?<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#when-is-a-copy-constructor-called\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>When an object is initialised from another: <code>Point p2 = p1;</code></li>\n<li>When an object is <strong>passed by value</strong> to a function.</li>\n<li>When an object is <strong>returned by value</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"destructors\">Destructors<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#destructors\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <mark class=\"kw\">destructor</mark> is a member function called <strong>automatically when an object goes out of scope</strong> (or when <code>delete</code> is used). It is named like the class with a tilde (<code>~ClassName</code>), takes <strong>no parameters</strong> and has <strong>no return type</strong> — it cannot be overloaded.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class File {\n    char *name;\npublic:\n    File(const char *n) {\n        name = new char[50];\n        strcpy(name, n);\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"Opening \" &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    }\n    ~File() {\n        delete[] name;\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"Closing file\\n\";\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    File f(\"data.txt\");\n    // ... work ...\n}   // destructor runs automatically here</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Typical uses: release dynamically allocated memory, close files, release resources (locks, sockets, connections).</p>\n<p><strong>Destruction order:</strong> destructors run in the <strong>reverse order of construction</strong> — the last object created is destroyed first.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If a class allocates memory with <code>new</code>, always write a destructor, a copy constructor and a copy assignment operator (the <em>rule of three</em>) to prevent double-delete and leaks.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"introduction-to-inheritance\">Introduction to Inheritance<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#introduction-to-inheritance\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><mark class=\"kw\">Inheritance</mark> lets a derived class <strong>reuse and extend</strong> the members of a base class. It models an <em>is-a</em> relationship (<code>Dog</code> is an <code>Animal</code>) and supports code reuse, extensibility and overriding.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Account {                       // base class\nprotected:\n    double balance;\npublic:\n    Account() : balance(0) {}\n    void deposit(double a) { balance += a; }\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; \"Balance: \" &lt;&lt; balance &lt;&lt; \"\\n\"; }\n};\n\nclass Savings : public Account {      // derived class\npublic:\n    void addInterest() { balance *= 1.04; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Savings s;        // constructor chain runs\n    s.deposit(1000);\n    s.addInterest();\n    s.show();         // Balance: 1040\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"types-of-inheritance\">Types of inheritance<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#types-of-inheritance\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Single</strong></td><td>one derived class from one base</td><td><code>B : A</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Multiple</strong></td><td>one class from two or more bases</td><td><code>C : A, B</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Multilevel</strong></td><td>chain of derivation</td><td><code>A → B → C</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hierarchical</strong></td><td>many classes from one base</td><td><code>B, C, D : A</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hybrid</strong></td><td>combination (e.g. multiple + multilevel)</td><td><code>D : B, C</code> where <code>B, C : A</code> — often needs virtual base classes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">// Multiple inheritance\nclass Student { protected: int roll; public: Student(int r) : roll(r) {} };\nclass Marks   { protected: int m;    public: Marks(int x) : m(x) {} };\nclass Report : public Student, public Marks {\npublic:\n    Report(int r, int x) : Student(r), Marks(x) {}\n    void show() const { cout &lt;&lt; roll &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; m &lt;&lt; \"\\n\"; }\n};</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Warning</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Hybrid inheritance can duplicate the base sub-object (the \"diamond problem\"). Mark the shared base as <code>virtual</code>: <code>class Student : virtual public Person { ... };</code> so only one copy of <code>Person</code> exists.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"overriding-base-class-members\">Overriding base class members<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#overriding-base-class-members\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A derived class can <strong>redefine</strong> a base member function with the same signature. This is <mark class=\"kw\">method overriding</mark> (distinct from overloading — same name, same parameter list, different class).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Animal {\npublic:\n    void speak() const { cout &lt;&lt; \"Some sound\\n\"; }\n};\n\nclass Dog : public Animal {\npublic:\n    void speak() const { cout &lt;&lt; \"Woof\\n\"; }   // override\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Dog d;\n    d.speak();            // Woof   (Dog's version)\n    d.Animal::speak();    // Some sound (forced base call)\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<ul>\n<li>If you hide a base function, its <strong>other overloads are hidden too</strong> — use <code>using Base::f;</code> to bring them back.</li>\n<li>Mark overrides with <code>override</code> (C++11) so the compiler checks correctness.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"public-protected-and-private-inheritance\">Public, protected and private inheritance<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#public-protected-and-private-inheritance\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>The base's members keep their access <em>up to</em> the mode of inheritance:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Base member</th><th>Public inheritance</th><th>Protected inheritance</th><th>Private inheritance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>public</code></td><td><code>public</code></td><td><code>protected</code></td><td><code>private</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>protected</code></td><td><code>protected</code></td><td><code>protected</code></td><td><code>private</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>private</code></td><td>not accessible</td><td>not accessible</td><td>not accessible</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Base { public: int a; protected: int b; private: int c; };\n\nclass Pub : public Base      { /* a public,  b protected, c hidden */ };\nclass Pro : protected Base   { /* a &amp; b protected,           c hidden */ };\nclass Pri : private Base     { /* a &amp; b private,             c hidden */ };</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">// Accessible from outside?\nBase b;\nb.a;   // OK\nb.b;   // error, protected\nb.c;   // error, private</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Tip</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Public inheritance expresses <em>is-a</em>. Private inheritance expresses <em>implemented-in-terms-of</em> and is rare. Protected inheritance is rarely useful.</p></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"effect-of-base-constructors-and-destructors-in-derived-classes\">Effect of base constructors and destructors in derived classes<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#effect-of-base-constructors-and-destructors-in-derived-classes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p><strong>Construction order:</strong> base class constructor runs <strong>first</strong>, then the derived class constructor. If the base constructor needs arguments, the derived class passes them in its <strong>initialiser list</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"cpp\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">C++</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">class Base {\npublic:\n    Base()               { cout &lt;&lt; \"Base default ctor\\n\"; }\n    Base(int x)          { cout &lt;&lt; \"Base(int) ctor\\n\"; }\n    ~Base()              { cout &lt;&lt; \"Base dtor\\n\"; }\n};\n\nclass Derived : public Base {\npublic:\n    Derived() : Base()         { cout &lt;&lt; \"Derived ctor\\n\"; }\n    Derived(int y) : Base(y)   { cout &lt;&lt; \"Derived(int) ctor\\n\"; }\n    ~Derived()                 { cout &lt;&lt; \"Derived dtor\\n\"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Derived d(5);\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">Output</span><button class=\"code-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy>Copy</button></figcaption><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Base(int) ctor\nDerived(int) ctor\nDerived dtor\nBase dtor</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Rules to remember:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Base ctor → derived ctor</strong> (base first).</li>\n<li><strong>Derived dtor → base dtor</strong> (destructors in reverse; derived first).</li>\n<li>If the base has only a parameterized constructor, the derived class <strong>must</strong> call it explicitly in the initialiser list, or the code will not compile.</li>\n<li>Each derived class constructor calls <strong>one</strong> base constructor; for multiple inheritance you list them comma-separated: <code>Derived() : Base1(x), Base2(y) {}</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"quick-revision\">Quick Revision<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#quick-revision\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>One-line meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Friend function</td><td>non-member granted access to private members</td></tr><tr><td><code>this</code></td><td>pointer to the current object inside member functions</td></tr><tr><td><code>new</code>/<code>delete</code></td><td>run-time heap allocation/deallocation</td></tr><tr><td>Static member</td><td>belongs to the class, one copy for all objects</td></tr><tr><td>Constructor</td><td>runs automatically at object creation (default/parameterized/copy)</td></tr><tr><td>Destructor</td><td>runs automatically at object destruction</td></tr><tr><td>Overriding</td><td>derived class redefines a base function with the same signature</td></tr><tr><td>Public inheritance</td><td>base <code>public</code> stays <code>public</code> (is-a)</td></tr><tr><td>Ctor/dtor order</td><td>base ctor first; derived dtor first</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Example</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><strong>Exam favourite:</strong> \"Trace the output of the ctor/dtor program above and justify the order.\" Construction is bottom-up (base first), destruction is top-down (derived first), because the derived object must be valid while the base part is being built, and must finish its own cleanup before the base part is destroyed.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice Questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#practice-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ol>\n<li>What is a friend function? Why is it not a member function? Give a program using a friend of two classes.</li>\n<li>Explain <code>this</code> pointer with a program that chains member calls.</li>\n<li>What is the difference between <code>delete p</code> and <code>delete[] p</code>?</li>\n<li>Write a class <code>Array</code> with a deep-copying copy constructor and a destructor.</li>\n<li>Explain static data members and static member functions with a <code>Counter</code> example.</li>\n<li>Differentiate constructor from destructor. When is a copy constructor invoked?</li>\n<li>List and draw the five types of inheritance. Explain the diamond problem.</li>\n<li>Show what happens to <code>public</code>/<code>protected</code>/<code>private</code> base members under <code>public</code>, <code>protected</code> and <code>private</code> inheritance.</li>\n<li>Explain the order of constructor and destructor calls when a derived class object is created and destroyed, with a program.</li>\n<li>What is the difference between overloading and overriding? Give one example of each.</li>\n</ol>"}]};
